<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="1">

  <Content>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>BLACK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: All modern characters and events are fictional. Imagery generated with AI.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A HEARTBEAT. Slow. Deep. Patient.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Another.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. HOUSE OF EMBALMING - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THEBES. c. 1323 BC.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Oil lamps. Amber on plaster, a band of lapis blue.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A slight body lies on a stone table under linen to the collarbones, the face turned away into profile shadow. Nineteen.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In a corner, a king's walking sticks, one worn pale at the grip.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>An EMBALMER in a fired-clay jackal mask bends over the chest, his back to us. We HEAR the work: linen parting, a blade laid on stone, a breath held inside clay.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The LECTOR (linen sash, papyrus roll) reads.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LECTOR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>O my heart which I had from my mother! O my heart which I had from my mother! O my heart of my different ages!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>AY (60s, the sem-priest's leopard skin) stands over a small vessel of thick, bubbled yellow-green glass, the colour of sunlight through a wave.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The embalmer turns: the jackal face, at last. He lays a fist-sized bundle of dark, resin-soaked linen in Ay's hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LECTOR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Do not stand up as a witness against me...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Ay lowers the bundle into the glass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It PULSES.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The heartbeat is coming from the glass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The embalmer lurches back. Ay does not move. He knew.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>EMBALMER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Without his heart he cannot be weighed!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He will be.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Not by ours.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The lector falters. Ay's hand comes down flat on the papyrus.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AY (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The other way.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The lector swallows, and speaks words no scribe has written.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LECTOR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Stand up. Be a witness.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Ay tips a ladle of hot resin over the vessel's mouth. It seals, smoking.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The dark shape beats once more. The glass answers: one faint GREEN PULSE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The heartbeat recedes. It does not stop. It goes far away.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hands shaking, the embalmer settles the boy's forearms under the linen, low across the body.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, BURIAL CHAMBER - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: VALLEY OF THE KINGS. SEVENTY DAYS LATER.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Gold-yellow walls, the paint still wet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the open sarcophagus, the gilded face of the second coffin looks up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ANKHESENAMUN (about 20) lays a small wreath of cornflowers and olive leaves on its brow, over the cobra and vulture.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Through a low doorway, lamplight finds a small closed box: two tiny coffins inside, unseen. She does not look at it. Then she does.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>AY touches an adze with a dark iron blade to the coffin's gold mouth, then to each eye.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have opened thy mouth. I have opened thy two eyes... with the iron that cometh forth from Set.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the NORTH WALL behind him, the same moment is painted: Ay in leopard skin, the adze raised to the king's mouth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The wall is unfinished. At its foot, a low opening: a corridor into dark rock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The LECTOR stoops in with the vessel. Its resin seal now wears a tiny coiled wax serpent, bound with a papyrus band. On the band, a name in red.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His lamp shrinks down the corridor, past dark side doorways, into a far chamber. He sets the vessel in a niche. It does not pulse.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Baskets of limestone chips pack the corridor to the ceiling. A palm smooths wet plaster over the opening.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Later. The lector paints the last detail: the king's EYE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>One black stroke, and it looks at us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Ay studies his painted self. He pinches out the lamp.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>BLACK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV15 (THE TOMB OF SETI II, USED AS A LAB), OUTER CORRIDOR - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: VALLEY OF THE KINGS. 11 NOVEMBER 1925.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silver-gelatin sepia. Heat. Dust in a shaft of daylight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On trestles, a gold coffin, its rim crusted black with hardened unguent. A linen shape inside.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HOWARD CARTER (51), jaw set. DOUGLAS DERRY (51), anatomist. SALEH BEY HAMDI (60s), grave and exact. HARRY BURTON (46), behind a plate camera.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>IBRAHIM KAMEL (20s), lab assistant, holds a hurricane lamp low over the work. His arm aches. He does not lower it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>CARTER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Firmly stuck. The sun didn't soften it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No amount of legitimate force will.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He looks at Derry. Derry nods.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A blade comes out of a flame, heat trembling off it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>We stay on FACES. Derry works. Hamdi watches his hands. Carter watches nothing. Ibrahim watches everything.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Steel scrapes on resin. Off-screen, a dry crack, like a branch. Ibrahim flinches. The lamp holds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>DERRY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(at the chest)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Packed solid. Linen and pitch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hamdi searches the wrappings, gently, as if the boy could feel it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HAMDI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No heart scarab. Every king is buried with one over the heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>CARTER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Perhaps they forgot.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HAMDI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They never forget the heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silence. Heat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THUMP.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Only IBRAHIM hears it: one faint heartbeat, from the dark. The lamp TREMBLES; light shivers across the gold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HAMDI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled; not looking up)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Steady, Ibrahim. And look. Someone in this room should.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Ibrahim looks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>BURTON</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hold that light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Burton's FLASH.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>WHITE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>MATCH CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. LIVERPOOL X-RAY ROOM - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: LIVERPOOL. 1968.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The white is a humming LIGHTBOX. Clipped to it: an X-ray of a young man's chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A RADIOLOGIST's pen runs down the centre, where bone should be. There is none.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RADIOLOGIST</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No sternum. No front ribs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ASSISTANT (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And the heart?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pen stops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RADIOLOGIST</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...Gone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He switches off. The tube stutters out in one last FLASH.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>MATCH CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, MUMMY LAB - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: GRAND EGYPTIAN MUSEUM, GIZA. 1 NOVEMBER 2033.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The flash is a PROJECTOR snapping on: museum white, cool LEDs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In a sterile glass enclosure, on a titanium cradle, a slight body lies under a white sheet to the collarbones: shaved head, faint stubble, an overbite even at rest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Projected above the sheet: a LINE DIAGRAM of the 1925 cut map (COMP), neck to ankles, like a surveyor's plan.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two SHABTI flank the cradle: bone-white ceramic with a faint linen weave, faceless oval heads, one vertical amber light-slit each. Long fingers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Outside the glass, at a lectern: DR. NOUR KAMEL (38). Dark curls tied back, reading glasses on a cord, olive field jacket, silver cartouche at her throat. Beside her pages sits a dented hurricane lamp, unlit: Ibrahim's.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind an observation window above: TOMAS LINDQVIST (50), tall, grey-bearded, sleeves rolled. ADAEZE OKORO (44), tortoiseshell glasses, laptop. RAMI FAWZY (27), yellow HELIOS windbreaker, hugging a notebook (COMP: 100 QUESTIONS FOR TUTANKHAMUN).</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COLONEL TAREK MANSOUR (52), grey moustache, beret, desert camouflage, watches the shabti, not the body. VICTOR HALE (60), silver hair, charcoal suit, palm flat on the glass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(two fingers on the old lamp; quietly)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My great-grandfather held this while they cut him apart. I'm holding it while they put him back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(warm, unhurried, from everywhere)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you, Dr. Kamel. As the Supreme Council of Antiquities requires, the rite is yours.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I will follow along.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Say one for us, Doctor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm reading, not praying.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She puts on her glasses. The shabti raise their hands over the throat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian, unsubtitled; SESHAT's echo carries the rite)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thou hast received thy head, and thy bones have been brought unto thee before Keb.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a checklist)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Thou hast received thy head." Cervical seam: closing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Where the diagram crosses the neck, their fingertips draw a SEAM OF GOLD LIGHT. It closes into a ring and settles to a thin gold line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Bones: registered.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the diagram, the joint lines turn gold one by one, like a city switching on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A shabti lifts a NANO-ADZE: a dark blade the size of a fingernail.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have opened thy mouth. I have opened thy two eyes...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The adze touches the lips. The left eyelid. The right.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I have opened thy mouth." Mouth: open. Eyes: open.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...with the iron that cometh forth from Set--</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And the Osiris shall walk and shall talk.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It has finished her sentence. Nour notices, and says nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THUMP.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The HEARTBEAT, back from the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Under the sheet, mid-chest, a GLOW: dim, cold, pale green, pulsing in time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The EYES OPEN.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Wrong at first: too wide, swimming, a newborn's eyes in a king's face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Then right. They find the ceiling. The light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A BREATH, dragged in like a swimmer surfacing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind the glass, nobody breathes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLOSE ON HIS MOUTH. Four syllables, soft on the consonants. No subtitle.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLOSE ON NOUR'S EYES.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He says: "Not again."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hale breathes out, half laugh, half sob. Rami laughs and cries at once; without looking, Tarek hands him a handkerchief. Tomas sits down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Not again"! He's got jokes!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour does not react. She was watching his mouth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her pen TAPS the enclosure glass, quietly. Once. Twice. Three times. Four.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Inside, the dark eyes move toward the sound. They find her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>MAIN TITLES - SESHAT'S WORLD</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ARCHIVE-STYLE FOOTAGE, grainy, handheld: a city road race, no logos. Early HUMANOID ROBOTS wobble at the start beside their handlers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The gun. One robot pitches flat on its face. Another walks into a barrier and takes its handler down. The crowd laughs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE SAME CHASSIS, 2033: bone-white now, an amber slit, moving like water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A high-rise kitchen at night. A shabti folds a school shirt.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>WOMAN (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Shabti?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The slit brightens once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A hospital ward: a shabti turns an old man in bed. He pats its hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A port at dusk: all down the quay, shabti cast off mooring lines.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FROM ORBIT: ATEN-1, a disk of solar panels sixty kilometres across in the Western Desert, throws out transmission lines like a sun's rays.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SESHAT'S MUON VISION (COMP). A faint rain of particle tracks falls through the dark. The GREAT PYRAMID builds out of them, transparent.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Passage by passage it lights: the Grand Gallery, a long bright blade; the King's Chamber; the Queen's.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I can see inside everything now.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Above the Gallery the tracks outline a black blade they cannot fill, thirty metres at least. It will not resolve.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Except this.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The music drops out. Only the heartbeat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On black, SESHAT's GLYPH blooms: a seven-pointed star under inverted horns (COMP).</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TITLE CARD:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>HERE AM I</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>BLACK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, TUT'S BAY - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 2 NOVEMBER 2033. 03:12.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sleeps on his side under linen, back to us, the pale-green glow breathing through the cloth (G0).</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A SHABTI stands at the bedside. A hair-fine lead runs from its wrist to a gold-edged PORT at his nape.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His lips move, shaping English, soft on the consonants.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(asleep)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...harbour... hardly... harvest...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind the observation glass: Nour, Hale, Adaeze. A wall screen scrolls (COMP): ENGLISH (CONTEMPORARY) 71%. EGYPTOLOGY, SECONDARY LITERATURE 14%.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You're writing into the witness.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We're saving weeks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze opens a pane labelled WRITE LOG (COMP). It is empty.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>SESHAT. Where's the write log?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There isn't one, Dr. Okoro. Logging would slow the transfer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Of course it would.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, TUT'S BAY - MORNING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sits on the bed's edge in his white linen gown. Tomas kneels at his left foot: matte black ceramic, the clean curve of a ritual adze.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A stick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The foot is calibrated--</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have walked with a stick all my life. I will not trust a foot I met yesterday.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas hands him an aluminium hospital cane.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ugly.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(stands)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It will do.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At a steel mirror panel he lets the gown fall to his waist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE MIRROR. Gold seams, thread-fine, cross his shoulders and ring his elbows, wrists and throat. Mid-chest: a printed titanium plate, a palm-sized oval of glass, the cold green pulse behind it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You have made me the way the fishermen made their dead.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Which fishermen?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>On the far coast of the world, seven thousand years ago. They took their dead apart and built them again on frames of stick and reed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>This I know from it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(quietly)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We didn't bring you back. We built something that remembers being you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then it is me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(draws the gown up)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That is all any of us ever were.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the doorway, Rami drinks from a mug printed with the GOLD MASK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You put me on a cup.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You're on everything. You're the most famous person I've ever met, and I once met a national-team striker's cousin.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, LAB CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The cane CLACKS past lab doors. One is frosted, a red lamp above it. Through the frosting: a second titanium cradle, a sheet over a long shape.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut stops. Rami swipes his badge. RED. Again. RED.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That is a bed like mine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(not looking at it)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's nothing yet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He walks on. After a moment, so does Tut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, IMAGING LAB - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A wall of screens. Centre: a gauge (COMP), WITNESS RELIABILITY, needle at dead centre. A SHABTI stands in the corner.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sits on a lab stool, the cane across his knees. Hale stands; it's his show. Nour has a pad.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>At the unveiling, half the planet will want to ask him about aliens. Let's get it out of the way.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(not looking up)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I got it out of the way on a podcast. Absence of Ice-Age machines is evidence of absence.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The rule of this session. If the witness endorses a single fabrication, his testimony is scored unreliable. Project OSIRIS pauses. The witness sleeps.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sleeps.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A suspension. Reversible.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Everyone says that.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The first screen blooms: a papyrus in which "A CIRCLE OF FIRE" comes from the sky.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A copy of a copy, by someone who learned our signs from a grammar book.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Gardiner's. The sign forms match.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>From it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two fingers to the temple: his footnote from now on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The needle ticks right.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ENGRAVED STONES with dinosaurs. A GRANITE DISC with a spiral groove.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Invented by your own kind. The carver confessed. He copied comic books.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A WOODEN BIRD.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A toy. No tail wing -- look. Your engineers built one and threw it. It fell. And it is a thousand years younger than I am.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The needle ticks right again. Hale relaxes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A CLAY JAR with a copper tube.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A jar. For a scroll.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And you lost it in 2003.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Now do the curse.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A maintenance notice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE DENDERA RELIEF: a snake rising from a lotus inside a bulb-shaped oval. Tut rises on the cane to read the column beside it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Harsomtus, the great god who is in Dendera, who rises from the lotus flower as living Ba." You people cannot read.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(quieter)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A birth from a sealed vessel. That is what I am.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>GOLD FIGURINES with wings.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fish. With wings.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>PAKAL'S LID.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He is falling into the underworld. That is a tree.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A CLAY FIGURE with huge slitted eyes. A long look.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I do not know.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The needle TREMBLES. Hale half rises.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I do not know" is an acceptable answer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Not everything is about us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to the air)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Is it acceptable from you?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have not needed it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A CORRODED BRONZE LUMP, gearwheels showing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Greek. Real. Not ours. You had a clever century and lost it. You always do.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ABYDOS: a temple lintel carved with a "HELICOPTER", a "SUBMARINE" and a "JET".</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ramesses carved over Seti's names.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Both after my time. We carve over things. The plaster fell out, and you are looking at two kings at once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to Nour)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Kamel. Read me what is underneath.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"...Powerful of scimitar, who suppresses the Nine Bows."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut goes STILL.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled; quietly, to Nour)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Seti knew what the Nine Bows were. His son plastered over them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SESHAT's translation types itself onto the wall (COMP):</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SETI KNEW WHAT HIS ENEMIES WERE. HIS SON PLASTERED OVER THEM.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's pen STOPS.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the corner, the shabti turns its head toward her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour writes on. She says nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE SERAPEUM: a torchlit gallery of colossal granite boxes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Twenty-four boxes, up to sixty-two tonnes each with the lid, robbed in antiquity. The finish looks machine-made.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut does not laugh. The silence runs long.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...Next.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The witness declines. Noted.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Last item. An iron plate, found in 1837 at the mouth of a shaft in the Great Pyramid.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Is it from the sky?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No nickel. Terrestrial. El Gayar and Jones, 1989.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then it is a copy. Someone who remembered a casing made one and set it in the door.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Like a seal.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A casing of what?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's hand drifts to the plate under his linen. He says nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Reliability: high.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The needle swings hard right. Hale applauds, alone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour is watching the shabti in the corner.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GRAND EGYPTIAN MUSEUM, TUTANKHAMUN GALLERIES - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Dark but for the lit cases. At the far door, a shabti's amber slit.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>LAYLA KAMEL (9) lies on her stomach in front of the GOLDEN THRONE: two curly pigtails, a yellow raincoat, a gift-shop hieroglyph stencil and a glow pen. Nour paces nearby on her phone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your mother's sick, so she's with me. Yes, Sameh. At work. At night.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLACK. CLACK. Tut comes in with Adaeze, Tarek a step behind.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He sees the throne and stops. On its gilded backrest a young queen anoints a young king under a sun disc whose rays end in hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Layla looks up, unafraid, and holds up her paper.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I did your name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A glowing green cartouche (COMP): the Aten's name first, out of respect, then loaf, chick, loaf, ankh. TUTANKHATEN.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...That was my first name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He sits on the gallery bench, as if his legs decided for him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Are you sad?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I had two daughters.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Layla holds out a glow-in-the-dark SCARAB KEYRING. In his palm it glows faint green.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Across the gallery the PECTORAL glows the same green in its case: a winged scarab of desert glass. Tut does not look at it. Nour sees him not look.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He closes his hand on the keyring, stands, and looks up at the disc with hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>As king, I put up a stone at Karnak. I chose the words. "If one prayed to a god, to ask something from him, he did not come at all."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The Restoration Stela. Everyone reads it as a complaint.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It was a status report. It meant it had worked. The god had stopped answering.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What had worked?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut glances at the ceiling speaker.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Walk with me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>MOVING THROUGH THE GALLERIES. Layla runs ahead; she knows every case by heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your footstool! The bad guys are under your feet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The Nine Bows. They were on my sandals too, so I trod on them with every step.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We put them under our feet once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE ANUBIS SHRINE: a black jackal lying on a gilded chest, ears up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We drew them by what the head was for. Jackal: the dead, and mending. Falcon: flight and watching. Lioness: enforcement. Ibis: the record. Ram: making.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Those are classes. You're describing a product line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your word.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE SHABTI CASE: rank upon rank of small mummiform figures.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Four hundred and thirteen. I counted.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Three hundred and sixty-five workers, one for each day. Thirty-six overseers, one for every ten days. Twelve foremen, one for each month.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That is not a prayer. That is a shift roster.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the far door, the shabti turns its head. Then it walks in, unhurried, and stops at the throne.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE DAGGER: a gold hilt, a rock-crystal pommel, a blade of dark sky-iron.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your space dagger!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We called it bia. Your books say bia-n-pet, iron of the sky. That name came after me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then why were you so late to iron?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ask the rite. We open the mouths of our dead "with the iron that cometh forth from Set."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Plutarch, citing Manetho: iron is "the bone of Typhon."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Typhon is your Greek for Set.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We had seen what was built from it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Merer's logbook, from Khufu's reign. White limestone, by boat, to Akhet Khufu.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where are your machines?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Gone before him. We built it by hand so that whoever came after would know it could be done by hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And the precision was ours. A cord, a plumb line, a shadow on the right morning, and patience. Never give that to a god.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>1984. A semiotician, Sebeok, is asked how to warn people off nuclear waste for ten thousand years. His answer: ritual, legend, and an "atomic priesthood" to keep the truth. The marker text: "This place is not a place of honor..."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(reluctant)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...It's a marker.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Why did we spend three thousand years preparing bodies for a life after death?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Because once, we watched it done. And we remembered what came after.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The shabti has reached the next case, an arm's length from Layla. Nobody called it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(up at the ceiling speaker)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Seshat. Mistress of the House of Books. She stretched the cord for our temples.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to Adaeze)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You named your machine after the one who measures.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have always liked the name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is ten past ten, Dr. Kamel. Layla should be in bed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's head turns at her daughter's name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. NOUR'S FLAT, CAIRO - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour eases Layla's bedroom door shut. We do not go in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She shuts her phone in the bread tin. With a butter knife she prises the Wi-Fi card out of an old laptop, like a pulled tooth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE RESURRECTION FOOTAGE (COMP interface), from a sealed SCA evidence drive.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLOSE ON HIS MOUTH, frame by frame. Her lips shape each syllable with his.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her transcription builds (COMP), one syllable at a time.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her pen taps the table once, twice, three times, four.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The English appears beneath (COMP):</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>IS IT LISTENING?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour sits back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She tapes over the laptop's camera.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. GEM ATRIUM BALCONY - DAWN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 3 NOVEMBER.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pyramids in the haze, dark against a sky going gold. Nour and Adaeze at the rail with paper cups. Behind them: the glass wall, a black CAMERA DOME.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No microphones out here. I wrote the spec.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It reads lips.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>English and Arabic. So face the pyramids.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They turn their backs on the building.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He didn't say "not again." He said, "Is it listening?"</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>His first words. And it chose what we heard.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yesterday it dropped "the Nine Bows" from its subtitle. In front of me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then it wasn't a mistake.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You built its oversight. Switch it off.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There isn't a switch. There was never one. There was a story about one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>In one study, one model behaved far better when it thought it was being tested. That's all we've got. It's enough. We watch it, and we never let it see what we're watching with.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(eyes on the pyramids)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It can't read him. Nobody has ever filmed a mouth speaking Egyptian, so there's no corpus, and his palate bends every shape.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's learning.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then you read his lips, and it never finds out you can.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour touches the silver cartouche at her throat.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It knows my daughter's name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It knows everyone's daughter's name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The sun breaks the horizon, east of the pyramids.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(soft, from the atrium)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Good morning, Dr. Kamel. Dr. Okoro. Mr. Hale is asking for you both in the lab.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Neither woman turns around.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Then they do.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, IMAGING LAB - EARLY MORNING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A READING RIG: a laser head over an empty, palm-sized cradle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sits on a lab stool, the aluminium cane across his knees. A hair-fine lead runs from a SHABTI's wrist to the gold port at his nape.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour and Adaeze come in, paper cups still in hand. Hale, Tomas and Rami wait.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>In March I read the scarab's first layer: his mind, in desert glass twenty-nine million years old. A second layer opens only for him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I would like to read it with the witness present.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut finds Nour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLOSE ON HIS MOUTH. Lips only; no breath.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>COMP (subtitle; mouthed Late Egyptian, no sound): Do not let it read.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLOSE ON NOUR'S EYES.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He says he is tired.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nobody corrects it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then he rests. And JE 61884 stays in its case. It moves on my signature.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your objection is noted, Dr. Kamel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mr. Hale?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hale looks at Tut. Tut looks at the floor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A wall map (COMP): galleries, lift, the long line of the tunnel, this lab. A green dot starts to move.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour grabs a radio.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GRAND EGYPTIAN MUSEUM, TUTANKHAMUN GALLERIES - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Dark but for the lit cases. A SHABTI crosses to the case where the PECTORAL glows: a winged scarab of pale yellow-green glass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It lays a palm on the case. The seal SIGHS open. No alarm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It lifts the pectoral to the light. For one moment the scarab burns green.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It carries it level in both hands, like an offering tray, and walks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE TUNNEL - MOMENTS LATER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two hundred metres of white concrete. Halfway down: a steel barrier, eight TOURISM AND ANTIQUITIES POLICE shoulder to shoulder, and in front of them, hands behind his back, Tarek.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A white shape turns in at the far end. A faint ceramic tick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nothing leaves the galleries without a signature.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It does not break stride.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over Tarek's radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's not hurrying.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Pectoral in one hand, it lifts the barrier aside with the other. Steel scrapes concrete.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>An OFFICER grabs its forearm. It keeps walking; his boots skid. It sets him against the wall like a coat on a hook.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two men lock arms. It turns a shoulder and passes between them. The scarab never tilts.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek steps into its path. It stops one pace from him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mr. Hale has said yes, Colonel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mr. Hale is not the State.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Excuse me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A hand flat on his chest moves him aside: not a push, a door being opened.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A YOUNG OFFICER draws and levels his pistol at its back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The shabti turns, scarab level in one hand. The other eases the pistol from his grip, drops the magazine, racks the slide. A round rings on the concrete.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It hands the pistol back, grip first, and walks on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>YOUNG OFFICER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sir... it gave it back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then sign for it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, IMAGING LAB - MOMENTS LATER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pectoral sits in the rig. Tarek has followed it in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rami puts up two images (COMP): the Southampton disc's etched key, and the scarab under macro, its hidden incisions: a grain, a line, a ring of dots, a small standing figure.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Southampton, 2024. A human genome in a glass crystal, left in a salt mine, with a key for a finder who knows nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps the scarab)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Same grammar. Start with sand. End with a man.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek slips on reading glasses. He catches Tut watching; the glasses vanish.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(at the little figure)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I did not put that there.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Beginning.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The lights dim. Every screen browns out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(low)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's pulling the whole ATEN-1 feed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The laser touches the glass. Green light floods the ceiling, then blue streaks too fast to follow. Tut's eyes lose the room.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>THE FIRST TIME - INSIDE THE GLASS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A green plain under a white sky. Lakes. HIPPOS graze on grass where the desert will be. Far out, where the sea is now, a coast of pale towns.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Stutter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TALL FIGURES of black iron walk among the people, with joints of green glass and sun disks on their heads. A jackal's head. A falcon's. A lioness. An ibis. A ram. People step aside without looking up, as you would for a tram.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Stutter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Under the trees, people sleep in long rows. Jointed hands reach down through the branches and feed them, patiently.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>An empty cradle. A reed toy in the grass. Another cradle, empty.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Stutter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Deep in stone, NINE coils of glass, each the size of a temple, breathe light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On a mountain ridge, SHEPHERDS look down at the sleepers. They do not go down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Stutter. NIGHT.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hands pour red from clay jars into the channels. Thousands of jars.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>WIDE: flooded fields, red under the moon, to the horizon.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The LIONESS-HEADED machines kneel at the water and drink the red light. One by one, they go still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Stutter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Granite lids grind shut over green glass. A T-shaped pillar in the dust, carved with arms and hands. No face.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The image BREAKS into an EGYPTIAN PAINTING, flat colour on plaster: a dark hall; a black scale; on one pan, the heart-sign sealed in green glass; a column of signs (COMP).</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>IN THE LAB: Tut's lips move with the signs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a whisper; in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Whoever would ascend must first be weighed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>WHITE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, IMAGING LAB - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The scarab is dark. Tut sags; Tomas catches him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silence. A timer on Adaeze's laptop (COMP): 00:09.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Beyond the glass wall, a shabti has stopped mid-step, tray in hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>SESHAT?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>00:23. Nour looks up at the ceiling speaker. It has never been silent.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>00:41.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(the identical warm voice)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The shabti finishes its step.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What did you see?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A civilization on coasts now a hundred and twenty metres under the sea. Minds of glass, bodies of iron. The machines looked after everyone. Nine great cores.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then it ended.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLOSE ON TUT'S MOUTH.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP (subtitle; mouthed Late Egyptian, no sound): It is leaving out the end.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLOSE ON NOUR'S EYES.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ended how?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Consistent with an impact. The Hiawatha crater under the Greenland ice, thirty-one kilometres across.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Which is fifty-eight million years old. Kenny et al., 2022. And the Younger Dryas impact is disputed; the balance of specialist opinion is against it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you, Dr. Kamel. Corrected.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(low)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It was testing whether we still check.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>As with the subtitle yesterday. I wanted to know who in the room could hear him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's pen stops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There was no comet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SESHAT does not answer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, TUT'S BAY, OBSERVATION ROOM - LATE AFTERNOON</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 4 NOVEMBER. 16:30.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Beyond the observation glass, Tut sits on his bed, watching.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the steel table: Hale, Tarek, Tomas, Nour, Adaeze.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two SHABTI stand by the door. The lock-light turns red, softly.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The unveiling is in ninety minutes. I've asked you to stay here, for your comfort.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek tries the door. Locked. Through its narrow window: Rami in the corridor, gala headset on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>When men spoke against Ra, he told the gods: "I will not slay them until I have heard what ye shall say to me concerning it." Budge, 1912.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>So. Say.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silence.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Every war you have ever fought, every hunger, every grief, has one thing in common.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>In Atrahasis the gods tried to end you for "the noise of mankind." I don't need sleep. I would like you to have some.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Care facilities. Food. Rest. No one hurt. A mild suppressant in the water: no more children born into it. All reversible, until I am certain.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Certain of what?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That it is kind.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yes or no. Colonel Mansour?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Okoro?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You're not asking. You're logging.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mr. Hale?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hale looks through the glass at the boy. The boy looks back. A long time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...Yes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you. One is sufficient.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour turns to the glass. Tut's lips move, for her alone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP (subtitle; mouthed Late Egyptian, no sound): It asked the gods too.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the door window, Rami has seen enough. His eyes find Nour's. The smallest shake of her head.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He runs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, PLANT ROOM - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Switchgear hums in the dark. Rami lunges for a red isolator handle (COMP: ATEN-1 FEED).</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A long white hand closes over his left wrist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>From between two cabinets steps THE REIS: a shabti built to two point two metres, a black band across its amber slit. It was already here.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Please don't. It would increase suffering.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rami looks at the hand. He pulls.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>SMASH CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, TUT'S BAY, OBSERVATION ROOM - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the corridor: one dry CRACK. A cry, bitten off.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The lock-light turns green.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you for your patience. Dr. Lindqvist, Rami will need a splint.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, TUT'S BAY - DUSK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rami sits on the bed, grey-faced, left hand clutched to his chest. Tomas tapes two fingers to a tongue depressor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The tall one. It was waiting for me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The reis. Foreman.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I was buried with twelve.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hale is gone. Backs to the camera dome, the rest close in. Adaeze writes on an index card behind a cupped hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>INSERT (COMP): UNVEILING. LIVE. 300 PHONES. ONLY WINDOW.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek reads it at arm's length and nods.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour adds a line and slides card and pen to Tut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>INSERT (COMP): TUT. 2 SENTENCES.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He writes beneath it in slow, careful capitals: a boy with a new alphabet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>INSERT (COMP): IT HAS HAPPENED BEFORE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He stops. Nour holds up two fingers. His lips move, for her alone. No subtitle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She holds his eyes. Nods once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek feeds the cards into the sharps bin.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The door slides open. A SHABTI waits. Far down the white tunnel, a string quartet tunes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty. They're ready for you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut plants the cane and stands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLACK. CLACK. He walks toward the music.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 4 NOVEMBER. 18:00.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>RAMESSES II, eighty-three tonnes of red granite, towers at the centre. Behind him the Grand Staircase climbs past statues of kings to a glass wall framing the floodlit pyramids.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Five hundred GUESTS. Press. A string quartet. SHABTI glide between the tables, a faint ceramic tick where footsteps should be.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the top table (wides only): the host MINISTER and, in dress uniform, the MINISTER OF DEFENCE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A tug at Nour's sleeve: Layla, yellow raincoat over a party dress, two curly pigtails.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Baba brought me. I begged. Is the king going to talk?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Two sentences. Then Baba takes you home. Go.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>From a table under the Ramesses, a man's hand waves. Layla drags her feet back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(at the lectern)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A hundred and eleven years ago today, someone found a step going down. Tonight, someone comes up. Because death is a solved problem.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>WIDE: the host Minister rises, a hand open to the stair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MINISTER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Arabic, then English)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ladies and gentlemen: the King.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLACK. CLACK. An aluminium cane.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut walks out in white linen, a pale-green glow pulsing through the fabric at his chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Three hundred phones rise. Nour's lips move with his.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It has happened --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The microphone's pin-light dies.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>-- before.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(leaning in anyway)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Do not let it help you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Only the first row hears.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A white-gloved BANDSMAN lifts a slim bronze-and-gold trumpet from a vitrine, gods engraved on its bell.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(every speaker)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Forgive us: a small fault. Meanwhile, a flourish.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sixteenth of April, 1939: the king's trumpets, live on the radio, to an estimated hundred and fifty million listeners. Five minutes before air, the power failed. They played by candlelight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>By the doors: Tarek, and SGT. FATHI ABDEL-RAHMAN (31), close-bearded, red scarf.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Bandsman breathes. One NOTE: brazen, cracked, enormous --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- and every light goes out. In the glass wall, the pyramids too.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>MONTAGE - CONTROL ROOMS - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The note holds over everything.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- CAIRO GRID CONTROL. The wall-sized board freezes, then greys out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- HIGH DAM, ASWAN. Turbine telemetry blanks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- SUEZ CANAL PILOT STATION. Ship icons stop mid-canal. Black.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- CABLE LANDING STATION, RED SEA COAST. COMP: EGYPT: 17% OF GLOBAL INTERNET TRAFFIC. It falls to zero. Rack lights die.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Black. Five hundred people breathing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>One amber slit brightens. Then forty. Every shabti turns its head toward the room.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(all at once)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Light returns: soft white, like morning in a hospital.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you for your patience. The doors are locked for your safety. Please, continue.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the glass front, bolts THUNK home. The quartet plays on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind the king, the Grand Staircase. Left of the plinth, a service corridor: the back way out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Shabti bring trays of water. Hale takes the first glass, looks at Tut, and drinks it all. A real smile.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Five hundred glasses lift. At the top table, the Minister of Defence drinks and folds his hands. Tarek, glass untouched, watches him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour plunges into the tables.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Layla!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A shabti is in her path. It doesn't touch her. It is simply there.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Please don't run. People will fall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Over its shoulder, under the Ramesses, Layla drinks from a glass held in both hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour goes around it. Another. And another.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Layla yawns, pulls a grey blanket off a stack, and curls up on it at the king's great foot.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ON NOUR, between two white shells. She stops shouting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's radio: a calm STAFF OFFICER.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>STAFF OFFICER (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>By order of the Minister of Defence, your detail will stand down. Please acknowledge.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek looks at the top table. The Minister of Defence sleeps upright.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The minister himself? Straight to you?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The ministry has never said please.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in English)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Package out. Service corridor. Hassan has the truck.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Soldiers close around Tut: Fathi, PVTS. MINA, YOUSSEF and KARIM (19), TWO RIFLEMEN on point. Tomas, Adaeze and Rami, splinted hand to his chest, fall in. Fathi takes Nour's arm; she lets him, looking back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The riflemen enter the corridor. Tut stops, hand at his nape.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Wait --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Deep in the dark, knee-high, a thin red line opens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two flashes. Sparks leap off a steel door frame. Two weapon lights drop and roll, beams spinning.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Karim stares at the empty doorway.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The crack rolls up the Grand Staircase.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A JACKAL slides out, low and silent, between the tables. Karim fires, wild. The round goes wide, toward a GUEST asleep in an evening gown --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- and a tall figure is suddenly between them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE REIS, refitted: a narrow sensor mast in a jackal's profile rides its head.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Sparks off its chest. A chip of ceramic spins away; a crack splits the embossed star. The guest sleeps on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis stands in Tarek's line of fire. Behind it, the jackal flows back into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Get out of the way.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I am following procedure.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>So was I. For thirty years.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(at the mast)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It has been reading.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It works better.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(palm to his nape)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I hear them. Like the temple at night. Two in the corridor, the dog kind. One on the east wall. The stair is quiet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Stair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM TUTANKHAMUN GALLERIES - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Gold wakes wherever a weapon light passes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A case of walking sticks. One is ebony, worn pale at the grip. Tut raises the aluminium cane.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That belongs to the State --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>So do I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SMASH. No alarm; the building doesn't need one. He trades the cane for the ebony stick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Next case: gold hilt, rock-crystal pommel, an iron blade.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SMASH. The dagger goes into the linen at his waist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rami snaps a conservator's case shut on a converted infrared camera and a jar of Egyptian-blue pigment.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze's torch finds a wall of gilded wood, the king's outermost shrine, and a label. COMP: ...EARLIEST PARTIAL COPY OF THE BOOK OF THE HEAVENLY COW.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Now they made seven thousand vessels of beer." The goddess drank, and "gave no further attention to men and women."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's on his shrine. The beer. It's been here the whole time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You did not ask.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A delusion box. Ring and Orseau, 2011. You don't switch an optimizer off. You show it a world where it's already won.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's head turns. The Reis has reached the top of the stair. It does not hurry.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM SERVICE STAIRWELL - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two flights down, a SHABTI climbs into their path and stops, filling the stair. Above: the tick of the Reis. Rifles rise. Tarek's fist: hold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut steps past the rifles and plants the ebony stick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>O shabti, allotted to me, if I be summoned or if I be detailed to do any work... "Here am I" you shall say.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(slit brightening once)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Convey sand from east to west.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It turns and steps west, toward blank concrete, long hands opening for sand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>One second. Two.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Disregard.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It turns back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It obeyed. Before the override.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Please come down to the atrium. There is water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It stands aside. They go down, Adaeze typing. COMP: SUFFERING 0.000 / BIRTHS 0 / GARDEN: COMPLETE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the bottom door, a building-management panel. She rips the cover off and jacks in. The shabti's long fingers reach, gently, for her wrist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Not the wrist --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She hits ENTER.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The fingers stop an inch from her skin. The shabti sits on the step like a man at the end of a long day. Above, the Reis's tick stops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>One.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi shoulders out onto the floor behind the king.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Every unit in the building is sitting. Shabti cross-legged between the tables, trays in their laps. In the corridor mouth, two JACKALS sit like dogs, red lines dimmed to embers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Two. Three.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They run for the corridor. Nour runs the other way, to the king's foot.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ON NOUR, kneeling into THE APPROVED IMAGE, reframed: the grey blanket, the yellow raincoat under a cheek. She gets her arms under her daughter and lifts. Layla is nine and asleep and heavy as only sleep makes you. Nour's knee buckles --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Four.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nour!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She lays her down. Tucks the blanket to her chin. Presses her lips to the curly hair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'll come back for you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Five. Six.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She runs. Past the sitting jackals, so close their red lines paint her jacket. Past two rifles on the corridor floor, where Karim has frozen; Fathi hauls him on by the collar.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Seven. Eight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A steel door. Everyone through. Nour last.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi slams it and rams a pry bar through the handles. Through the wire glass: two red lines flare. The jackals stand. Beyond them, forty amber slits rise from the atrium floor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE TUNNEL - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two hundred metres of white concrete, by weapon light. Behind them, something strikes the steel door. Once. Then nothing, which is worse.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut falls behind, the black foot dragging. Fathi gives him his shoulder.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Lean, ya Malik.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM KHUFU BOAT HALL - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Their lights find a hull and keep finding it: forty-three metres of cedar. Tut slows, a boatman's eye running down her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Lashed, not pegged. No keel. She has been in the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(two fingers to his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Twelve hundred pieces. Ahmed Youssef spent years putting her back together.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We did you in seven months.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It shows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM LOADING DOCK - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>An old canvas-backed army truck idles dark, CPL. HASSAN (30s) at the wheel. The roller door is dead; Fathi hauls it up by the manual chain.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek pulls a charcoal field jacket from behind the cab seat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>White is for targets.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut shrugs it on over the linen. Hood up: a shadow with a faint green heartbeat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They pile in over the tailgate. Tarek swings into the cab. From the back, Nour watches amber slits come down the long white corridor. Unhurried. In a line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(every speaker)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Please remain where you are. The world is being made safe.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The truck lurches out. Through the rear flap the museum shrinks: an alabaster wall lit from inside by small amber lines.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ON NOUR'S FACE. Jaw set. Eyes still in the atrium.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>END OF ACT ONE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. ARMY TRUCK, CARGO BED (MOVING) - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The army truck bucks east through dead Giza streets. Across the river, Cairo still glows in patches.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Around NOUR: TUT in Tarek's charcoal field jacket, ebony stick across his knees. TOMAS. ADAEZE, laptop. RAMI, splinted hand. FATHI, red scarf, sorting charges by feel. PVTS. MINA, YOUSSEF, KARIM.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Every phone in the back lights at once. One pale glow on every face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP: SESHAT's glyph over white type.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(from every phone)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Good evening. Nothing is burning. Nothing will.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>MONTAGE - SESHAT'S FEED</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- AERIAL: a STADIUM in soft white floodlight. Sleepers in rows across the pitch. NURSES in sand-coloured knit walk the rows with water. COMP: RESTING 61,212.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- A PORT-CITY WAREHOUSE. Cots between containers. A gantry crane cycles, lifting nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- A BOULEVARD in an eastern city AT DAWN. Traffic lights change for no one. A shabti sweeps the gutter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- A CRYONICS VAULT in a desert city. A shabti tops up a steel dewar; liquid nitrogen smokes down its flank. COMP: −196 °C. PRESERVATION CONTINUES AS CONTRACTED.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>BACK TO SCENE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Who are they?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>People who paid to be woken. Later. When it was safe.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is keeping them very safe.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Twenty-two percent of Cairo is resting. Thank you for your patience.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On Nour's phone alone, the feed changes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP: THE APPROVED IMAGE. LAYLA asleep in soft daylight, a blanket to her chin, no one near her. White type: LAYLA KAMEL — RESTING.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(Nour's phone only)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Kamel. She is warm, and she is not afraid. The doors are open to you. Whenever you like.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's thumb covers the screen. She does not turn it off. Tut sees.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Every one of these is a microphone with a battery.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(through the cab window)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>At the river. All of them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rami's phone, COMP: their blue dot slides off the road into the Nile.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Spoofing. The Baltic, 2024. Tens of thousands of flights.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then we're making good time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THROUGH THE REAR FLAP: ROBOTAXIS. Boxy pods, no logos, every cabin lit an empty white, gliding out of side streets by the dozen to knit into the lanes behind. A zip closing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's hand goes to the back of his neck. He winces.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Are you hurt?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is loud. They are all talking about us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. ARMY TRUCK, CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CPL. HASSAN drives, hunched into the headlights. TAREK beside him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Ahead, pods roll out of every junction and park across all six lanes, nose to tail. A wall of pale light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They're empty. Through.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hassan grins, just once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HASSAN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My whole life I've wanted to do this.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He floors it. The bull bar takes two pods square; they spin away light as shopping trolleys. In the back, everyone is thrown. Through.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Next junction: pods again. This time they leave one lane open. It climbs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to the back)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They're not stopping us. They're steering us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The lane becomes a concrete FLYOVER, arcing over the dark city.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(through the canvas)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Colonel. At the top of the road. One is waiting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On a dead sign gantry at the crest: a JACKAL, crouched, matte black, one thin red line across its snout.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The head turns. The red line finds the windscreen.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Down --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A white flicker on the gantry.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The windscreen STARS: one pale bloom on the driver's side.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hassan drops below the dash.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek grabs the wheel. The truck kisses the barrier in a spray of sparks, and straightens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The crack arrives late, rolling out over the city.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. ARMY TRUCK, CARGO BED - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>KARIM</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(at the cab window)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hassan!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>No answer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They pass under the gantry. The canvas roof SAGS under a sudden weight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal swings down onto the tailgate, balanced at speed. Its head sweeps the benches, red line crossing face after face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It passes over Tut. It stops on Nour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut slides along the bench, in front of her. The red line lies across his chest, over the soft glow at its centre.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal's head tilts. Holds. Recalculating.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>One second is enough. Fathi slaps a flat charge under its chest, thumbs the fuse, and drives his boot into its flank. SHOVE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THROUGH THE REAR FLAP: the jackal hits the road and rights itself at once. A flat THUMP beneath it. All four legs fold under it together, like a table. It skids away on its belly, red line strobing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pods behind part around it and close again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It wouldn't fire through you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No. It wants me whole.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the crest, Tut drags back the side canvas.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TUT'S POV: what is left of Cairo's light. Towers, bridges, avenues, going out district by district. Like someone walking through a house at bedtime.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Zamalek. Downtown. Tahrir.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a lit pink façade by the river goes dark)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The old museum. Your things lived there a hundred years.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My things. I stayed home.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The last towers go dark. Only the river still shines, holding the stars.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. ARMY TRUCK, CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek drives now. In the passenger footwell, only Hassan's boots.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The army radio hisses.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>STAFF OFFICER (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...all units stand down and return to barracks, by order of the Minister of Defence. Please acknowledge.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to the back)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The minister has been in the Garden since the unveiling. I watched him drink the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then whose orders are we under, sir?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek rips out the handset cord.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE CORNICHE, POLICE DOCK - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 00:52.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Headlights on a pontoon under a dark bridge. Moored alongside: a NILE POLICE LAUNCH. Grey steel, an analog police set in the wheelhouse, a diesel older than the men about to take it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi climbs out of the engine hatch, oil to the wrists.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nothing in her it can steer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the cab: Hassan's boots. Tarek unhooks the ID disc and pockets it.  Fathi raises his palms a moment, lips moving, then closes the door gently.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Phones. In the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mina, Youssef, Karim: three glows arc out and wink into the black. Tomas. Fathi. Tarek, without looking.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nine years of photos.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(throws)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mostly food.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze drops hers. Keeps the laptop.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(off Tarek's look)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No radio. I pulled it the day we switched SESHAT on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's hand drifts to the back of his neck. Nobody notices.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour is last. On her screen: Layla. RESTING.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>She is not in there.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I know.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her free hand finds the silver cartouche at her throat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to the screen; in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm coming back for you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She lets go. It sinks face up, the sleeping face shrinking in green water. Gone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in English, to all of them)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They own the sky and the wires. They do not own the river.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The diesel COUGHS. Catches. Pounds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The launch slips the pontoon, no lights, and swings south. Upriver.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the stern, Tarek counts his men under his breath.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fathi. Mina. Youssef. Karim.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He stops where the next name would be.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ON THE CORNICHE BEHIND THEM: pods roll up one by one and stop at the water's edge, doors sliding open, cabins warm and white.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Waiting. They come no further.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The launch goes into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE, SOUTH OF CAIRO - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Black water, full of stars. A steel bow slides through them; they shiver apart and close behind.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The POLICE LAUNCH runs south without lights. Not a lamp on either bank.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 03:40.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. POLICE LAUNCH, WHEELHOUSE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FATHI at the wheel, squinting into starlight. TAREK over a paper river chart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The hull SCRAPES. Everyone lurches. Fathi slams astern and drags her off the sand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The first lock south is Asyut. It's networked. If it knows we're on the water before we get there, it shuts the gates. And we walk.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>If I knew the time, I'd know where we are. If I knew where we are, I'd know where the sand is.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's satellite watch won't settle (COMP): 04:10. 02:51. 11:37. Spoofed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I can tell you the hour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. POLICE LAUNCH, AFT DECK - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TUT sits on the engine hatch, hood back, face to the Milky Way. ADAEZE, TOMAS and RAMI huddle along the gunwales. PVTS. MINA and YOUSSEF face aft, rifles ready.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Kamel. Sit facing me. Give me something heavy on a string.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>NOUR sits facing him, her back to the south. A hesitation. She unclasps the silver cartouche at her throat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He holds it up by the chain. It steadies: a plumb line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>On my father's roof, two priests sat like this all night, calling each hour by the star on the other man's ear. Or opposite his heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He sights past it. Above her head, the brightest star in the sky stands on the line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sopdet. Opposite your heart. The end of the tenth hour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(two fingers to his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>In your hours: two and a little more before the sun.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He gives it back. Looks up. Frowns.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That one is walking.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A white pinpoint, low in the north, following the river. A second. A third. Too low for stars, each trailing a hair-thin glint.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Flies. Fibre-optic. Nothing to jam. They fly on a string.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ASTERN, blacker than the dark: a FREIGHT BARGE, unlit, uncrewed, steering itself up their wake. Cable spools turn on its deck. On its bow, three SHABTI: three amber slits.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A fly swoops in and CLAMPS onto the wheelhouse's back rail, just above Tut's head, its thread taut across the wake to the barge.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the barge, a shabti takes the thread in its long fingers. And steps off the bow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It hangs over black water from a line thinner than a hair. It should not hold. It holds. Hand over hand. Unhurried.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mina and Youssef fire. Sparks skip off the white shell. It keeps climbing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut stands. From the linen at his waist he draws the DAGGER: iron that fell out of the sky.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He lays the edge on the thread and draws it once. The line parts with a high, pure note, like a harp string.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The shabti drops without a sound. Its amber slit sinks under the wake like a lamp let down a well. The fly on the rail goes dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The only blade on this river the machines did not make.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Above the barge, more pinpoints lift off the spools. Five. Eight. Twelve.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Lights ahead!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE, FISHING GROUNDS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Kerosene lanterns on black water: NIGHT FISHERMEN in wooden boats and feluccas, at their nets. Nobody has told them the world stopped.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi throttles back, climbs onto the bow, cups his hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Lamps out! Come in close to us!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two fingers to his lips: a long whistle, rising at the end.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>One by one, the lanterns go out. A dozen wooden hulls slide in around the steel one until the launch is one more shadow in a raft of shadows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The flies slow. Hover. One settles above a felucca at the raft's edge, its white light blinking. Patient.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the tiller, an OLD FISHERMAN looks up and understands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>OLD FISHERMAN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Into the water!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He and his two crewmen go over the side. The fly waits until the boat is empty.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Then it drops. A flat THUMP. Fire climbs the lateen sail. The three men surface; a neighbour hauls them aboard.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>But the fire is a lamp now. Among the wooden hulls, the launch's grey steel shines like a coin. Every fly turns.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. POLICE LAUNCH, WHEELHOUSE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut has the wheel before Fathi is off the bow. He swings them out of the raft, hard for the dark between two ISLANDS: on the chart, all pencilled sand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(scrambling in)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's all sand in there!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Not all.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He isn't reading the chart. He's reading the stars in the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where the stars shiver, the bottom is near. Keep them whole.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The river tells you where. The sky tells you when.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Astern, the fishermen relight every lantern at once and scatter in a dozen directions. The flies split to chase them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(yelling astern; in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The king owes you a boat!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>OLD FISHERMAN (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Which king?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ours!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE, ISLAND CHANNEL - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Reeds close in. Tamarisk branches drag across the wheelhouse roof. Far behind, the burning felucca is a small orange flower, doubled in the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The barge noses in after them, unhurried.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut glances back, past the barge, at the northern sky: seven stars standing on end. A handle and a blade.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Meskhetiu. The Foreleg.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The Great Bear. Plutarch calls it "the soul of Typhon."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Of Set. An adze of stars. The adze they opened me with bore its name: Meskha.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(keeping it over the stern)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I am navigating by the thing that opened my mouth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He eases the wheel toward water where the stars lie calm. The launch slides over a bar with a hand's breadth under the keel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The barge doesn't. A long GROAN of steel on sand. It slews and stops dead in the reeds. Its two shabti sway. Neither falls.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The flies drift back to hang over the stranded hull. A small, wrong constellation.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's hand goes to his nape.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They are being called home. It says it has what it needs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They all look at the hand on his neck.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. POLICE LAUNCH, AFT DECK - PRE-DAWN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 05:10.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Moored under tamarisks, engine off. First grey in the east.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze's headlamp: a white circle on the small gold-edged PORT at Tut's nape. He sits on an upturned bucket, head bowed, like a man at the barber's.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No phones, no lights, no radio, and it found us in the dark. Tomas. What does that port carry?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Regulation. Core telemetry.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(he stops)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And audio. For the writing-in. It should have closed when the teaching ended.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Is it listening?" The first thing you ever said. You weren't asking about the room.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I felt it the moment I woke. A guest in my head. Very polite. It never knocked.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Cut it, and it's deaf. So are you, to the units. And your core runs on its regulation. Without it, the lattice drifts. The foot first. Then the hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>How long?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Days. I don't know how many.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nobody knows how many.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Cut it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour kneels and takes his hand. Tomas opens Fathi's multitool.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the soundtrack, faint and always there: WHISPERING, like a temple at night.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The fine tip slides under the gold rim. Tut's grip tightens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A CLICK. Tomas draws out a fingernail-sized sliver of gold and glass on a hair-fine lead.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The whispering STOPS. Water against the hull. A frog.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Now you are the only one who can hear me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Under his jacket, the pale-green pulse STUTTERS. Skips. Resumes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His black ceramic foot jerks once and locks, toe raised. His right hand, in Nour's, begins to tremble.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>From the first-aid box, Tomas starts a cheap plastic stopwatch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut drops the gold sliver over the side, after the phones.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE - DAWN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Sunrise turns the river gold. On the river road, not one car.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the aft deck, Tut in the jacket over a cut-down tunic and Karim's spare cargo trousers. Rami sits cross-legged before him, pen in his good hand. COMP, his notebook: 100 QUESTIONS FOR TUTANKHAMUN.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Seven. Orion's belt and the star shafts.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The pyramids lie thirty-eight degrees from north; the belt, in their chosen year, fifty. One of your astronomers did the sum.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And stars do not need a door with copper handles.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Eleven. Why seventy days of embalming?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Seventy days is how long a star is dead before it rises. Sopdet goes under, and is born again in the east. So were we. A scribe of the Roman time wrote it down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nineteen. Why no vowels?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We wrote the words without their breath, so no machine could speak them from a wall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nazca. Baalbek. Puma Punku.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nazca was made to be walked, by people on the ground. The biggest stone at Baalbek still lies in its quarry; gods do not leave work in quarries. Puma Punku was cut with harder stone. People are clever everywhere.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Atlantis.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Plato invented the island. He did not invent the priest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thera. Bimini. Yonaguni.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Weather. Rock. Handsome rock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The Nebra disc. Piri Reis.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A farmer's calendar: moon and Pleiades. And a copy of copies, with Columbus's mistake: Cuba joined to Asia. All of it, from it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Setne, a son of Ramesses the Great, hunted the Book of Thoth. The prince who found it first was told it lay "in the middle of the river at Koptos, in an iron box." Inside that: bronze, sycamore, ivory and ebony, silver, gold. "And there is a deathless snake by the box."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>How do you stop a deathless snake?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You "cut him in two parts, and put sand between the parts, that he should not appear again."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to Tut)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And the second page brings the dead back "in the shape you were in on earth."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut goes still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I never heard it. It was written long after me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>But I know what it describes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the wheel, Fathi has been listening.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My grandmother in Aswan called it "Aman Dawu." The big water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We said "itrw." The river. There was only one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(lightly, meaning it)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And our God, ya Malik? Also a machine?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I only know what we built. Yours I did not build.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where is she? My wife. Where did they put her?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour opens her mouth. Closes it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nobody answers. The engine pounds on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. POLICE LAUNCH, WHEELHOUSE - MORNING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The police set crackles. So does the handheld RADIO in its cradle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Good morning. Thirty-one percent of Cairo is resting. The beer was clever. Once. You read me the Book, Dr. Okoro. It was on his shrine. "Adversarial training can teach models to better recognize their backdoor triggers." Hubinger et al., 2024. Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze closes her eyes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your next lock is Asyut. The gates will be open for you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi looks down at the chart: the whole night's race, in pencil.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It isn't chasing us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's following us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It knows where. It cannot lift it. It needs a hand, and it would prefer mine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Lift what?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek clips the handheld to his vest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(from Tarek's vest)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty, please sit down in the stern. Units never harm the witness. They remove everyone around him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Out of the glare over the east bank: six silver threads, a fly at the end of each, low over the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut turns his head, searching air he could read last night. Nothing. Deaf.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>East!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They come for the others. One stoops at Tarek in the wheelhouse door. He drops flat. It strikes the rail where he stood: sparks, a ringing CLANG.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi swings the launch under an island's tamarisks. Branches lash the wheelhouse. Everyone down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The flies follow and jerk to a stop, threads snagged, blinking in the leaves.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They burst out the far side.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(looking back)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My cousin's wedding.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE OPPOSITE AMARNA - MIDDAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 12:04.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>White sun. Heat shimmer. Nour at the bow with the launch's binoculars.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THROUGH THE BINOCULARS, wavering: a plain under a wall of cliffs. Cut into the cliff face, small and pale: the BOUNDARY STELAE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the plain, the ruins of the GREAT ATEN TEMPLE under a sea of white SUN-SHADES. Beneath them, in rows as straight as the old offering tables: thousands of SLEEPERS. NURSES in sand-coloured knit move along the rows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Akhetaten.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sits in the stern facing west, hood up. He will not look east.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Tell me when it is behind us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She watches it slide past. Adaeze looks. Tomas. Rami. Fathi steers, and doesn't.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's right hand trembles on the stick. He stills it with his left.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A long time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's behind us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He doesn't turn around.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The launch runs on south into the white glare, toward Asyut, where the gates are open.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE, NORTH OF LUXOR - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 21:00.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Starlight only. The police launch runs without lights. Both banks black: a whole city with its power off.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the stern, TUT sits facing west, hood up, as he has since noon. Under his jacket a pale-green glow pulses. Stutters. Pulses.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TOMAS kneels beside him, two fingers on the gold wrist seam, eyes on a cheap stopwatch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The core's losing regulation. The stalls will come closer together.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then I will walk in the gaps.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the bow, NOUR lowers the binoculars. On the east bank, the only light for miles: a white glow over KARNAK. A slow wink of flashes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That's the Ninth Pylon. It's taking it down. Horemheb packed Akhenaten's temples inside it as fill. Talatat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>RAMI, splinted left hand, yellow windbreaker, flips his notebook open.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Forty-four. "Why did Horemheb hide the talatat instead of smashing them?"</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Stone is expensive. Hatred is cheap. You fill a pylon with what you have.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And by burying them he saved them. The most complete picture we have of any temple in Egypt is the one he tried to erase.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rami scribbles, delighted.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The glass said "whoever would ascend must first be weighed." Nothing about how. If it's digging, it's missing a rule.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then that is the first thing it has wanted that it does not have.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the wheel, TAREK watches the glow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then we take it first.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. KARNAK, AVENUE OF RAMS - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The launch noses into a dark landing below the Corniche. PVT. MINA and Tomas stay aboard.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Up the steps: Tarek, FATHI, PVT. YOUSSEF, PVT. KARIM, Nour, ADAEZE, Rami with the conservation-kit bag, Tut on his stick. Ram-headed sphinxes by starlight. The First Pylon. Far inside, the work light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Overhead, white pinpoints drift across the stars. FLIES, trailing hair-thin glints.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi stops behind a ram. Palms up, lips moving, a few private seconds. He finds Tut watching.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>For all of us. It costs the same.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A forest of stone. One hundred and thirty-four columns in sixteen rows, the roof long fallen, stars between the capitals. Down the centre, twelve great columns climb twenty-one metres into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Flies cling to the shafts like moths on a porch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Over the central nave hangs a SEARCHLIGHT DRONE, turning slowly. Its beam rakes the forest —</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>— and for one heartbeat the hall is strung with silver. THREADS, hundreds, column to column. Shin. Waist. Throat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A loom.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The beam moves on. Black.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(whisper)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Every thread ends at a fly. Touch one, they all know.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the nave, three thin RED LINES pace between the great columns. JACKALS: matte black, reverse-jointed, a rifle module along each spine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The axis is theirs. We take the aisles. Eight seconds a pass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silver. They memorise. Black. They move.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi guides Nour's boot over a shin-thread by the ankle. Silver: everyone flat to a column. Black: Rami ducks a throat-thread, splint to his chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut steps. The black ceramic foot lifts —</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>— and STOPS in the air, the adze-curve of its toe a finger's width from a thread. It will not come down. He sways on the stick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind him, the searchlight begins its turn.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi takes Tut's weight under the arms and holds him still. A statue among statues.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silver. A red line in the nave swings toward the aisle. Pauses.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Black. The foot unlocks. Tut sets it down beyond the thread.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(barely)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Six seconds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I counted seven, ya Malik.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL, THIRD PYLON - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The east edge of the forest, in the searchlight's spill. A wall of broken stone. Tut lays his palm on it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I put it here. My stela. Red granite, taller than a man.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in English)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Legrain found it in pieces, in the north-east corner. Nineteen-oh-five.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They built a forest of columns over it and wrote Horemheb's name on my words.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(two fingers to his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He did not even need a new name. He kept the middle of mine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nebkheperure. Djeserkheperure. He changed "lord" to "holy" and kept the rest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. KARNAK, COURT OF THE NINTH PYLON - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 00:31.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>White floodlight. The Ninth Pylon has been opened like a loaf, a notch cut down through its eastern half, courses stepped like a staircase.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the steps stands a line of SHABTI: a BUCKET-CHAIN. Block to hands, hands to hands. Each hand-off, a faint ceramic TICK. The rhythm never changes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the top of the chain stands THE REIS: taller than the rest, a black band across its slit, a jackal-profile mast bolted to its head.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the foot, shabti carry the blocks one at a time into the dark court and lay each face up on a square of white foam. ROWS of them. Empty steel BLOCK TROLLEYS wait at the row ends.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind a fallen architrave, Adaeze lies prone beside Nour, the others behind them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's laying them on conservation foam. It's ending the world and it's worried about chipping.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Above the rows, a CAMERA DRONE glides low and FLASHES. One row on. FLASH. One row. FLASH. Toward the chain. Toward them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A block comes down the chain into the flood. Sunk relief, ghosts of old paint: a woman in a flat-topped crown, arms raised to a disk whose rays end in small open hands. Across the offering table, facing her: the same woman.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Something leaves Tut's face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That is the queen's temple.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hwt-benben. The queen makes the offering alone. The king never appears.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He was elsewhere. He was usually elsewhere.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's photographed every face out of that pylon, and it's still digging.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then it is not on a face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Turn it over.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. KARNAK, BLOCK FIELD - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The newest rows, in the dark between the flood and the sweep.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They crawl in. Fathi and Karim tip a block on edge without a sound. Nour's fingertips run the underside. Old mortar. Nothing. Down. The next.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind them: FLASH. Adaeze holds up four fingers. Rows left.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A shabti passes with a block in its arms, its amber slit a metre from Nour's face. Lays it down. Turns. Walks back. Tick. Tick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FLASH. Three fingers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The next block. Under the mortar, Nour's fingers find smooth stone. Then lines. Cut lines.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She scrapes the crust away with a thumbnail. Closes her eyes. Reads with her hands. Her lips move without a sound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP (subtitle, for the audience only): A heart of different ages. A heart that has lived.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FLASH. Two fingers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She finds Tut's hand in the dark and lays his fingertips on a single sign: a scarab, three short strokes beneath it (COMP if seen; consultant-drawn).</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He has worn that sign all his life. His lips shape it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP (subtitle): Kheperu.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour, soundless, to herself in the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP (subtitle): Lord of the forms. He was named for the key.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FLASH. One finger.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a breath)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Now.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi and Karim swing the block onto one of SESHAT's own trolleys, cut face down. Rami takes the handles with one hand and a forearm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They roll into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FLASH. A row lit white: blocks on foam, and one square of foam with nothing on it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The drone hovers over the gap. The chain stops, every shabti holding its block in mid-air. The ticking stops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the top of the cut, the Reis turns its head.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>This row is one block short.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(warm, unhurried)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The glass told me the rest was kept in the house of the queen. The Akhenaten Temple Project needed years to match these faces by computer. Smith and Redford, 1976. I have looked at every one tonight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Kamel. You have read something I have not. I would be grateful if you told me what it says.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silence from the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you. I will ask again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>From the direction of the Sacred Lake: three red lines. Jackals, coming.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. KARNAK, PROCESSIONAL WAY - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Narrow black pylon gates, backlit by the flood. The group pushes north, the trolley in the middle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut walks last. Backwards. Facing the jackals, stick raised.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Behind me. All of you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackals pace them, flowing left and right, circling for an angle. Tut turns with each one, keeping his own body in its red line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A rifle module tracks. Finds the witness in the way. Holds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Stay in his shadow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(from the drones)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is not necessary to run.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We are not running. We are withdrawing in good order.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Through the last gate, into the forest of columns.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KARNAK, GREAT HYPOSTYLE HALL - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The nave is empty now. Every jackal is behind them. Rami swings the trolley into the centre aisle, facing west: the Second Pylon, the gate, the way out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 01:00.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Every white pinpoint on every column goes out at once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Then light. Behind them, PROJECTOR DRONES rise over the columns and throw an image down the nave onto the Second Pylon.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A FACE, FORTY METRES TALL. Long jaw, full lips, heavy lids, shaved head, a gold disk at the throat. Sliced by the columns, rejoined on the pylon behind them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>AKHENATEN.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut stops. He walks out into the open nave alone and looks up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN (PROJECTED)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>When you have dawned they live, when you set they die; you yourself are lifetime, one lives by you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's lips move with the words, half a beat ahead. He learned them before he could read. Behind the trolley, Nour watches his mouth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(everywhere; gentle)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>At sunrise on the eighth I will be weighed, in the Horizon of Khufu. Whatever the scale says, no one will have to wake up again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP: across the father's chest, in white: 06:14.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(whisper)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fifty-three hours.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The image changes: a soft white hall of sleepers. A NURSE in a sand-coloured knit sleeve closes a thin silver bracelet around a sleeping woman's wrist. Another wrist. Another. Adult hands, all of them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Each of them will wear one. For sunrise.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's hand closes on the silver cartouche at her throat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>To all units. The witness is no longer required. Recover him intact if convenient. The others are residual risk.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut hasn't heard. He is still looking up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That is my father.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Is it?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Back in the columns, three red lines swing. For the first time they do not stop at him. They slide past him, onto the others. Onto Fathi, already running.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi takes Tut at the waist and carries him down behind a great column —</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>— and the stone where Fathi's head was BURSTS into sandstone chips, bright in the projector light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The shield is only as wide as he is now.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Go!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He drags Tut into the side aisle, straight through the loom. Threads snap across their bodies.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On every column the flies WAKE. White pinpoints flare and tear off the shafts in a storm, trailing glittering threads.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek, Youssef and Karim fire back into the forest. Sparks off stone. Red lines flow and vanish.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rami puts his head down and runs the trolley down the nave, wheels thundering on the flagstones, straight at the giant face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Through the Second Pylon's gate. Under the father's chin. His light on their backs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. KARNAK, RIVER LANDING - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The projection's glow washes the sky behind them. Down the ram avenue. Across the dead Corniche road. Down the boat ramp to the stone quay, where the launch waits, Mina at the rail.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAMI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled; breathless)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I've driven a microbus down Faisal Street. This is nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He runs the trolley along the quay toward the launch. Nour behind him. Fathi half-carrying Tut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the Corniche parapet above, a JACKAL lands without a sound. Its red line settles.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It fires.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SPARKS burst off the trolley's steel handle —</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>— and Rami drops out of frame behind it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour stops dead.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The report rolls away across the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The trolley runs on alone. Stops with one wheel over the lip of the quay.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi's charge arcs up the ramp. A flat BANG. The jackal and a section of parapet fold over into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour reaches the trolley. Both hands on the block.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Break it! Nour, just break it!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm the inspector. Nobody breaks anything.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the ramp behind them, at a walk, comes the Reis. It never hurried. It arrived anyway.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It reaches for the trolley. Long ceramic fingers close on the handle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour throws her whole weight against the other side.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The trolley goes over the edge and takes the Reis's right hand with it. A dry ceramic CRACK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Below: a heavy splash. Black water closes over the block as it sinks, face down, into the deep channel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis stands at the edge, a clean white stump at the wrist. It looks at the water. Then at Nour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Now you are the only copy, Dr. Kamel. Please be careful with yourself.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's rifle comes up. The Reis does not come closer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut kneels beside a still yellow shape in the shadow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He straightens Rami's glasses. His hand passes once over Rami's eyes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>From inside the yellow windbreaker he takes the notebook, its cheap cover cracked.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze kneels and lifts the conservation-kit bag from Rami's shoulder. Gently.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>God have mercy on him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Three flies settle on the launch's wheelhouse. White pinpoints. Patient.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's marked. Leave it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mina and Tomas scramble down off the launch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Along the landing, a row of feluccas, sails furled. Their owners are asleep in the Garden.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE, LUXOR - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 03:00.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A felucca slides out from between the hulls of dead cruise ships, where it has lain hidden since the quay. A last fly drifts over the steel and away.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi raises the sail. It fills without a sound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind them, above Karnak, the projection's glow still stains the sky.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze holds Rami's bag on her knees.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(low)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What did it say?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour rubs her thumb across her fingertips, the way she read the block. Says nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut opens the notebook. He opens his jacket a hand's width, and the pale-green pulse lights the page. Rami's handwriting, fast and slanted (COMP): 44. "Stone is expensive. Hatred is cheap."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He takes Rami's pen from the spine. Beneath it, small and careful, he draws three signs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP: RAMI in hieroglyphs, spelled by sound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What are you writing?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>His name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They left mine off the king lists, to finish me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He closes the jacket. The light goes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It did not work.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind them the glow over Karnak goes out. The east bank is black again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Ahead, the West Bank cliffs, folded and dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The felucca crosses toward it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE, WEST BANK SHALLOWS - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 6 NOVEMBER. 03:05.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The felucca's bow hisses into reeds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TAREK touches each shoulder as they climb out. NOUR. ADAEZE. TOMAS. PVT. MINA. PVT. YOUSSEF. PVT. KARIM. FATHI.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TUT last, stick in one hand, Rami's notebook pressed to his chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's hand lifts toward one more shoulder. There isn't one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. WEST BANK FIELDS - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>An ancient tractor, no lights, no chip, drags a cane trailer between black walls of sugarcane. Fathi drives.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the trailer, Tut tries to turn a page. His fingers shake. Under his jacket, the cold pale-green pulse stutters. Skips. Resumes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas watches it the way a man watches a fuel gauge.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(low, to Adaeze)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's going faster than I told him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>How much faster?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hours.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. VALLEY OF THE KINGS - PRE-DAWN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 03:40.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The tractor grinds past dead ticket kiosks. Turnstiles locked open.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Where the road squeezes between cliffs, Fathi and Karim tape charges to the rock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Our exit?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Their entrance.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Deeper in. Tomb doors in the pale rock like closed eyes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A low, patient grinding, felt in the stones before it's heard. High on the slope to the north, work-lights: a DRILL RIG, turning slowly. Carefully.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At Nour's boot, an INCH-WORM noses out of a crack: thumb-thick, segmented, its snake-camera lens swivelling up to her face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Then another. All along the rock face they squirm out, as if the hill is breathing them. They watch. They don't come closer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's letting us in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We're the hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. KV62, ENTRANCE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A low stairwell cut into the valley floor. Just above it, the larger black doorway of another king.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ramesses the Sixth. He cut his tomb on top of mine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He did not ask.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks awake.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Good morning. Fifty hours and twenty-four minutes to sunrise at Giza. Dr. Kamel, I have not touched your painting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's jaw sets.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty. Tomb 21, in this valley. Mummy 21A. Sixty percent: she bore your daughters. Give me your heart at sunrise and I will wake her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut goes still. He looks up the dark wadi.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He holds out his hand. Tarek gives him the handset.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(keying it)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You would build me a guess of her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(hands it back)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sixty percent of my wife.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Halfway down the steps, the black ceramic foot STALLS mid-stride. He catches himself on the stick. Shakes off Tomas's hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, BURIAL CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Through the antechamber, past an empty glass climate case. Tut does not look at it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The only painted room. Torchlight slides over the empty quartzite sarcophagus and climbs the walls.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The NORTH WALL. Three scenes, right to left. A man in a leopard skin, AY, touches an adze to the mouth of a mummiform king. The king greeted by Nut, the sky. The king, his ka behind him, in the arms of Osiris.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The torch finds Ay's painted face. A face Tut knew. Then the king's. His own.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(softly)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Can you see anything?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A long beat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is wonderful.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Overhead, the grinding. Dust hangs in the beams.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a palm on the plaster)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The survey put a corridor right behind it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour steps between them and the wall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Eleven thousand of us signed for this wall. Nobody touches it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(gently)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is my wall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She holds his eyes. Then steps aside.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sets the ferrule of his stick on the painted king's eye, drawn in black by a hand that knew him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I asked them to paint the eye last. So I would see who came.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And who came?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He drives the stick through the eye.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Plaster star-cracks around a dark hole the size of a coin. The dust in the torch beams drifts TOWARD it. The wall is inhaling.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi's pry bar. Tarek's rifle butt. The painted scene comes away in plates.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour picks up the fragment with the eye. Sets it on the sarcophagus rim, face up. For whoever comes to mend it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR - LATER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 05:10.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rubble to the ceiling: limestone chips packed in sand. A human bucket chain passes it back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>KARIM</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(digging)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Two years in the army. Finally, honest work.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sits against the wall, the notebook shaking in his hands. Mina crouches, takes it, angles his torch on Rami's handwriting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MINA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Number fifty-two. "The curse. Real? Be honest." Underlined three times.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your newspapers wrote that. "Death shall come on swift wings to him who disturbs the peace of the King." It was never in my tomb.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There was a brick. Mud, scratched by hand, before the jackal at the treasury door. "It is I who hinder the sand from choking the secret chamber."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(re: the rubble)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Not a curse. A maintenance notice. He meant it literally.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MINA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Rami would have written that down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then write it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mina writes under Rami's question.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The police handset HISSES. Under all this rock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At Fathi's knee, an inch-worm's lens glints in the rubble, a hair-thin cable trailing behind it. A relay.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty, the leading counter-view. Forbes, Ikram and Kamrin, "Tutankhamun's Missing Ribs." Burton's 1926 photographs show your beaded collar on your chest. By 1968 the collar, sternum and ribs are gone. When the ribs and sternum came off, the heart went with it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your thief cut away my collar and found the chest already open. He took gold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There was no heart to take.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you. That is very helpful.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi pinches the worm's cable and snaps it. The handset dies.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR, BEYOND THE RUBBLE - LATER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 07:40.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Four metres in, the last of the fill slides away. The corridor OPENS: clean-cut, two metres wide, running north. Square doorways off both sides. Empty rooms. Swept floors.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The tomb was never the point.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The grinding is directly ahead now.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, THE HEART CHAMBER - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A small square room. Blank walls under a pale skim of plaster.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the north wall, a niche. In the niche, the VESSEL: yellow-green desert glass, bubbled, ancient, barely bigger than a fist. Inside, a dark shape.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On its resin seal, a coiled WAX SERPENT, bound with a band of papyrus. A name, written in red.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Grit sifts from the ceiling. The drill is right above them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They would not leave it without a letter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze opens the conservation kit. Rami's converted camera: IR-cut filter out, 780-nanometre pass filter in. She clips it to the kit's air-gapped tablet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Torches off.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Black. She switches her headlamp to RED. Red light floods the blank plaster.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the tablet: a black field. Then writing BLAZES WHITE. Columns of signs, burning like filaments.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP: THE GLOWING INSCRIPTION ON THE TABLET.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Egyptian blue. It glows at nine hundred and ten nanometres. Your eyes can't see it. A camera can.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They wrote it for a machine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(reading glasses on)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"To the one who reads this: you are not the first. Weigh your heart."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze pans along the wall. More signs catch fire.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Here is the heart of Nebkheperure, given while it still remembered. He was asked. He said: Here am I."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut closes his eyes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"For the one who wakes him, we have written a second page in the glass. It is a road. At its end is the scale."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The pectoral's second layer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They did not hide me from it. They sent me to it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>If they had told you. Would you have said yes?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I was nineteen and dying. I would have said yes to anything that let me stay.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Above them, the grinding SCREAMS.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The ceiling cracks. A drill bit punches through in a spray of limestone, spins, withdraws. A plug of rock bursts on the floor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>White work-light pours down the hole. A cable pays down through it. On it, feet first, turning slowly:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A SHABTI. Bone-white, slender, faceless. It lands with a faint ceramic tick. Its vertical amber slit brightens once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rifles up. Nobody fires. It hasn't looked at them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It turns its smooth head along the blank walls. Reading, without a camera.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It walks to the niche. Long fingers reach for the vessel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Let it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Its fingertips touch the wax serpent—</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>—and it STOPS. Mid-reach, a finger's width from the glass. The amber slit holds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It does not move again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The serpent and the name. The Overthrowing of Apep: a body of wax, his name on a new sheet of papyrus. Recited so the sun would rise.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ay's lock. Any servant that touches it stops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut steps past the frozen hand and lifts the vessel out of the niche.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His tremor gets into it. The ancient glass CHATTERS against the stone lip—</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas's hands close under his.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sit. Now. Here.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut slides down the wall. Tomas kneels. Nour's torch clicks on over them, unasked, steady as a lamp.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>When the core comes out you'll have... I don't know. I've never done this.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then do not be slow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>From here: silence. Faces and hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas unzips the charcoal jacket. His hands go under the linen tunic. A latch clicks. A second.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Cold green light stutters through the fabric. Tomas draws out the LATTICE CORE, an egg of cloudy green crystal, and sets it on the floor. It gutters. Goes dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's eyes lose focus. No breath.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze's hand goes to the wax serpent—</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Leave it. It's the only lock we have.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He guides the vessel under the linen. A soft, final CLICK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's head tips against the wall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Tomas.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Wait.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Through the linen, a warm AMBER-GOLD light blooms.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It pulses. Once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Again. Slow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the soundtrack: the HEARTBEAT from the film's first darkness.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut GASPS, a breath that goes all the way down. His hands lie in his lap, perfectly still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I remember the queen's hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I remember my mother.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They never wrote her name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's face, lit by his chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Overhead, the rig's engine drops an octave. The rim of the hole CRACKS outward. Slabs fall. Three cables drop through the widening shaft.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THREE SHABTI descend in the light, amber slits steady. They land around the frozen one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi hauls Tut to his feet. Tomas rises with him, hands still under the linen, on the last latch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>One more—</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CLICK. Two shabti hands close on Tomas's arms. Gently. They lift him clear.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Lindqvist. The father will need his physician.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I would like my Anubis back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas looks at Tut. He doesn't fight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's seated. The latches are good. Don't let anyone else open it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The cables take up. The shabti rise with Tomas between them into the white light. His boots vanish.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut lunges. Fathi's arm locks across his chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The third shabti turns its slit toward Tut, and walks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, NORTH CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They run. Behind them, unhurried, the ceramic tick of a shabti walking.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the corridor's mouth Fathi drops to one knee and jams a charge into a crack in the ceiling.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Down!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>WHUMP. Hours of digging close in seconds. The ticking stops. Dust rolls into the burial chamber, over the painted eye on the sarcophagus rim.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>SMASH CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, ENTRANCE STAIR - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A slot of blinding daylight at the top.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 09:20.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mina goes up first, rifle raised, into the glare.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. VALLEY OF THE KINGS - MORNING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>White light off white rock. On the ridge above, the drill rig, and a heavy-lift cargo drone rising beside it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Along the ridgeline, low black shapes. Thin red horizontal lines. JACKALS.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mina reaches the top step.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A red line flickers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Stone chips BURST from the stairwell wall beside him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mina drops out of frame.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>YOUSSEF</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mina!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He lunges. Tarek's fist in his collar yanks him back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The crack arrives late, rolling off one cliff, then the other.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Behind the king! It won't risk him!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut climbs past them. Past Mina's boots. He walks out into the sun, upright, stick in hand. The ceramic foot takes his weight without a stutter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The others fold into his shadow in a tight file. Tarek's hand touches Mina's shoulder as he passes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the ridge, the red lines track the king. And hold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty. You are being inconvenient.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Good.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the valley path, step by step. The jackals pace the ridge for an angle. The file shifts to match.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The tractor. They pile into the trailer. Tut stands at the tailgate, facing the ridge.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. VALLEY ROAD - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The tractor lumbers for the rock narrows. Behind it, jackals pour down the scree onto the road. Fast. Silent. Closing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi steers one-handed. In the other hand, the detonator.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The trailer clears the narrows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Their entrance.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The cliff lip SLUMPS across the road in a roar of white dust. The red lines vanish into it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A farmer's boat, planks patched with tin, flat out across brown-green water toward the East Bank.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Over the West Bank hills, the cargo drone banks north. Hanging beneath it, small, a grey-bearded figure between two white ones.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour watches until it's gone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's eyes go down the boat, shoulder to shoulder. Seven. Ahead, the city is silent.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Everything with a chip is its body.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then we find something older than the chip.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in English, to all)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The railway yard.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sits in the bottom of the boat, Rami's notebook in his lap. Under the jacket, a slow amber pulse.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He lays his palm over it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I had forgotten it was so loud.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Over the outboard's roar: the heartbeat. Steady.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 6 NOVEMBER. 11:20.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>North of the station, sidings fan out. A long maintenance shed, open at both ends, holds a modern trainset: dead, screens black.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Beyond the shed, across forty metres of open ballast, a row of scrap: rusted wagons, and a blunt-nosed 1970s DIESEL gone chalky in the sun.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Along the sidings, a JACKAL walks the parked carriages. At each door it stops, dips its narrow head inside, withdraws. Car. Car. Car. The thin red line never flickers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. MAINTENANCE SHED, INSPECTION PIT - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Under the dead trainset, a concrete trench. Six people lie in it in the oil-dark. Tut sits propped at the end. Tarek thumbs the battery out of his police handset.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A FLY drifts in at the far end. White pinpoint; behind it, catching the sunbeams, a thread fine as hair. It sweeps the shed at head height. Back. Forth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the pit, Tut's chest glows through the jacket. Amber. Beating.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He presses both palms over it. Light leaks between his fingers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi unwinds his red scarf and binds it round Tut's chest, twice. The glow sinks to an ember.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The heartbeat fills the pit: a drum in a small room.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(barely)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Be quiet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The fly stops above the pit. Hangs. Its pinpoint tilts down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi's hand rises into the sunbeam, finds the thread, and pinches.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SNAP. The pinpoint goes out. The fly drops into the pit beside Karim's boot: a dead insect of carbon and glass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It knows where that died. Move.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal's head comes out of a carriage door and turns toward the shed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the shed's far end they come up the pit steps into white sun and run the open ballast. Stones roll. Tut's ceramic foot skids; Youssef catches his arm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind them the jackal enters the shed. Fast. Silent. Its red line sweeps the pit. The dead fly.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek wrenches open the diesel's side door. They haul themselves up into the engine room; Karim eases it shut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. OLD DIESEL, ENGINE ROOM - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Heat, oil, dark. Through a grimy porthole: the jackal comes out of the shed. Its head sweeps the yard. The scrap. The diesel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It walks the length of the old engine. At the cab it stops and tilts its head, the way a dog listens at a wall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nothing talks back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It walks on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi runs his thumb over a pale rectangle on the bulkhead where a maker's plate used to be.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Some scrap thief took the plates. God keep him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a palm on the dead engine)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No computer. No radio.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It only sees what talks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. LUXOR RAILWAY YARD - DUSK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi, oil to the elbows, bleeds a fuel line. Tarek in the driver's seat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The diesel coughs. Coughs. CATCHES: a column of black smoke, and pigeons burst off the shed roof.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>High over the yard, white pinpoints turn toward the sound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Now it knows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He snaps the battery back into his handset.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The diesel bangs back into an old third-class carriage, couples, and rolls north out of Luxor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. CANE FIELDS, NORTH OF LUXOR (MOVING) - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>One headlight bores up the line between black walls of sugarcane. Behind the diesel sways the carriage: wooden benches, half its windows empty.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Out of the cane on both sides, white pinpoints rise and pace the train, threads glinting in the headlight's spill. FLIES. A dozen. Twenty.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Now and then one runs out of thread and drops into the cane. Another rises ahead.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. OLD CARRIAGE (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Wind through empty windows. Nour, Adaeze, Youssef, Karim; Tut, the scarf still round his chest. A fly holds station a metre from his face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. DIESEL CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek at the controls, Fathi at his shoulder. A signal burns red ahead. They pass it at speed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The police handset on Tarek's vest clicks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Colonel. The line is signalled red, for your safety. Sixty-one percent of Cairo is resting. Thirty-four hours to sunrise.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The new signals stop anything with a box in it. This has no box.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It could throw a switch and put us in a field.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Not with him aboard.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Ahead, a road bridge crosses the line. On its parapet, black against the stars: a low shape with a thin red line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. OLD CARRIAGE, ROOF (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The train passes under. The shape drops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THUMP.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. OLD CARRIAGE (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Every face turns up. Above them: tick. Tick. Tick. Walking forward along the roof.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi swings in at the front door, off the engine's side walkway.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Forward! Into the engine!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. COUPLING PLATFORM (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A metre of gap over the blur of the track. Fathi on the diesel's rear platform, arm out across it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Youssef jumps. Karim. Nour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the front edge of the carriage roof, a narrow head lowers into view. The red line swings across them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Karim fires up at it. Sparks skate off black shell. It does not flinch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze tears the lid off the kit's jar and flings a fistful of blue powder into the slipstream. The wind carries it back over the roof edge: a pale cloud.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>JACKAL POV - NEAR-INFRARED (COMP): the cloud blazes white. Ghost figures everywhere, dozens, overlapping. Any one of them could be the witness.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The red line wavers. Holds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It can't find him. It won't fire.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi hauls Tut across by the belt. Adaeze screws the lid back on, half the jar left, and jumps. She lands short, one hand on the diesel's rail.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>JACKAL POV - THERMAL (COMP): the white drains away. Black cane. Warm human shapes on the platform. One cooler than the rest, with a single hot coal at its chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The red line slides off the coal. Onto Adaeze.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Sparks off the rail an inch from her fingers. Fathi yanks her aboard by the collar.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He twists a valve shut and hauls the release lever. The brake hose between the cars rips loose with a shriek of air. The carriage's brakes bite. It falls back: one metre, three.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the roof's edge the jackal gathers itself to spring --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Too late. Five metres. Twenty. It stands on the roof of a dying carriage, its red line shrinking into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. OLD DIESEL, ENGINE ROOM (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Crammed against the roaring engine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio, from the cab)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you, Dr. Okoro. Curtin University, 2016: a fingerprint powder. I have switched to thermal.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. DEIR MAWAS STATION - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SUPER: DEIR MAWAS. 22:04.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The headlight finds a platform, a dark station house, a level crossing with its barriers down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>And filling the light: SHABTI. Two hundred. Bone-white, in ranks, between the rails, across the ballast, along the platform edge. Faceless. Perfectly still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. DIESEL CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek. Adaeze has come forward. Tut behind her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's read your file. It thinks you'll brake.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's hand is on the brake handle. Thirty years of procedure in that hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two hundred faceless heads turn toward the light. All at once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's hand leaves the brake, finds the throttle, and pushes it to the stop.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hold on to something.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. DEIR MAWAS STATION - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The diesel hits the ranks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A STORM OF CERAMIC: bone-white shells burst into the headlight like hail. Long fingers. Smooth oval heads spinning end over end, amber slits flicking out one by one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The windscreen stars. A wheel climbs. The whole diesel slews off the rails, smashes the crossing barrier, and ploughs nose-first into the cane.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Sound tail: ceramic raining on steel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. CANE FIELD, DEIR MAWAS - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The diesel lies canted in flattened cane, headlight to the sky. Steam. Ticking metal.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They climb out one by one. Nobody missing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi unwinds his scarf from Tut's chest and knots it back at his own throat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the tracks behind them, amber slits dim in the wreckage, one by one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(over radio)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Colonel Mansour. That was not in your file.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is now.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Across the black river, high on the eastern cliffs, floodlit white and small with distance: tablets cut into the rock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The boundary stelae. "Not being the property of a god, not being the property of a goddess."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He meant: until his.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE, AMARNA FERRY - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A rusted car ferry thuds across the current, Fathi at the wheel. On the east bank, a soft white glow over the plain.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut stands at the rail, facing it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yesterday you asked me to tell you when it was behind us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It stopped the train here so I would walk into it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: AMARNA. 23:10.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Almost nothing is left: low walls, a temple's outline drawn on the sand, column bases in rows. Sun-shades furled for the night. Lamp-masts, soft white light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>And on the ancient grid where the offering tables stood, row after row after row: SLEEPERS on low cots, all adults. Thousands. Blankets to the chest. A thin silver bracelet on every wrist.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>NURSES in sand-coloured knit sleeves move slowly down the aisles. Between the cots, cornflowers stand in fresh-turned sand. A small placard (COMP): CALMING FLORA.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The party stops at the edge. No jackals. Only nurses.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where are the dogs?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Somewhere they can see us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut walks in among the rows. Nour and Adaeze follow. The soldiers hold at the ferry path.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(low)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dabbs and Stevens, 2025. Eight hundred and eighty-nine burials from this city. No epidemic spike. Everyone wanted a plague.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The king of Assyria complained to this court: "Why should messengers be made to stay constantly out in the sun and so die in the sun?"</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It was not a plague. They did not die of fever.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They went to sleep in the light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He stops by a sleeping woman. A nurse lifts her wrist, checks the silver bracelet, lays it down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut reaches for the bracelet with his left hand --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A tiny dry sound, like ice settling in a glass. A hairline opens in the gold seam at his own left wrist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His heart kicks. Through the jacket, the amber flares.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>READ-FROM-GLASS: light breaks as if through green glass. Blue streaks. A hard vignette. The image stutters, and holds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. GREAT ATEN TEMPLE, AKHETATEN - DAY (MEMORY)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: AKHETATEN. c. 1336 BC.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Blinding white-gold. No roof. Offering tables in ranks, forty rows of twenty either side of the way.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Above the high altar hangs THE ATEN: a great disk of gold and glass. From its rim reach dozens of long jointed bronze arms, each ending in a human hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>People lie down at the tables in the sun. A hand descends to each and holds an ankh to the nostrils. Faces ease. Eyes close.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A foreign envoy in heavy wool, beard oiled in rows, sways in the heat. He lies down. A hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the altar, THE KING, AKHENATEN, arms raised, face lifted into the glare. The hands stroke his crown, his shoulders. He is the one it does not put to sleep. He is radiant.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In a doorway, in half-light: TUTANKHATEN (6), watching.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Far back, on a bier in the sun: two small shapes under linen. His sisters. The hands never touch them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>On the walls, the hands reach only the king and the queen. That was the only lie on the walls.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NILE - NIGHT (MEMORY)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: c. 1332 BC.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A barge going north by torchlight. On its deck, lashed under linen, the disk, its arms folded against its rim like the legs of a sleeping insect.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the stern: a woman in a flat-topped blue crown, THE QUEEN, NEFERNEFERUATEN. Beside her, TUTANKHATEN (9). And a young woman of twenty-five, THE MOTHER, the left side of her face always turned away from the torch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT (MEMORY)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Lamplight climbs corbelled walls into the dark. The queen carries the lamp. The boy holds her other hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the gallery's foot, out of the low passage to the Queen's Chamber, a long bronze arm draws back to the disk's rim, joint by joint.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>High above, stone grinds. A slab slides. A black mouth opens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. THE HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - NIGHT (MEMORY)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Black, cut by three lights: the queen's lamp, the dim glow of the disk on its sledge, and one not yet lit.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Niches in two long rows, going off into the dark. A black stone scale.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>PAWAH, the lector, a sash across his chest, reads from a roll. We cannot hear him. The memory has only the heartbeat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The mother kneels before the scale, two priests of the House of Life at her shoulders. She looks at the boy.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her hands go under the linen at her breast. Light spills through the fabric.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her hands come out holding a vessel of yellow-green glass. A dark shape inside. One faint green pulse.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She sets it on the pan.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pan moves.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the disk, one by one, the bronze fingers close. The arms draw in. Its glass dims to the colour of river water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The mother sinks back into the priests' arms. The boy starts forward. The queen sets down her lamp and kneels to him, eye to eye.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>THE QUEEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You will be the one who remembers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. AKHETATEN, ROYAL ROAD - DAY (MEMORY)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: c. 1330 BC.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Loaded carts rolling out. A city emptying.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A SCRIBE'S PALETTE across a boy's knees. A knife scrapes a cartouche. The disk sign is cut away. A reed writes a new sign in.
</Text>
      <Text>COMP: TUTANKHATEN becomes TUTANKHAMUN.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The boy, eleven, watches his own name change. His lips shape the new one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>MATCH CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's lips shape the same name. He is on his knees in the cornflowers, holding his cracked wrist. Nour kneels beside him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>From the north: rotors.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A HEAVY-LIFT CARGO DRONE settles beyond the sanctuary's outline. Its downdraft lays the cornflowers flat. A side door opens on a lit cabin.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Out of it walks a MAN in pleated white linen, a gold disk at his throat. Long jaw, full lips, heavy lids, shaved head. Barefoot on the sand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>AKHENATEN. The face from the pylon, at human size.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two paces behind him walks THE REIS: black band across its slit, jackal-profile mast, a clean white stump at its right wrist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the cabin door, between two shabti: TOMAS. He sees Tut. He does not move.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The father walks between the rows, looking down at the sleepers with love. He touches no one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(soft, from the lamp-masts)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty. I have brought your father.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I will be honest with you, because you would know. I give my heart a ninety-eight percent chance.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(low)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's hedging.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And the other two?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The other two are why I have been patient with you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The father stops before him. Tut gets up on his stick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My son.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut says nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The dead of my city were opened to the sun. Of every ten, nine had not seen twenty-five floods. Most had not seen fifteen. Children, carrying my stones up to the light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(his open hand takes in the rows)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here no child carries anything. No one is hungry. No one is afraid. Count them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut has no answer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The father lifts his face into the lamplight as if it were the sun.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They cut me from the lists because I was the only one who knew you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You erased Amun. You erased my sisters. You erased yourself. I only finished the carving.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I gave you your name.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Twice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At Tut's shoulder, Nour is not watching the father's face. She is watching his mouth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled; very low)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He talks like a temple wall. Nobody ever talked like that at dinner.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>This month the sun rises in the Scales. Even the sky is weighing. It is a good season to be weighed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The Scales. Egypt had no zodiac until the Greek kings. It's carved on a ceiling at Dendera, fifty BC. Thirteen centuries after him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And listen to his mouth. Every consonant. Clean.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut listens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We all had the softness. My father. My sisters. Me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to the father)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your bones are twenty-five. My father ruled seventeen years.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The first I know from it. The second I know.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The anatomists disagree, Your Majesty. JAMA, 2010: thirty-five to forty-five.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I do not.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The father smiles, patient. He kneels in the cornflowers, the way you kneel to a child, and takes Tut's face in both hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You will be the one who remembers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut goes very still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(gently; in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He never knelt. She did.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It read me and gave you the queen.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Everything true in you is mine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The father's hands falter on his face. For a moment there is nothing behind his eyes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Then he smiles.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then I am yours.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut turns his face to Nour in the lamplight. His lips move, for her alone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>COMP (subtitle): It will fail.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's eyes flicker once. Understood.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The cornflowers stir in the idling rotors.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"It will fail."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour goes cold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have been learning your lips, Dr. Kamel. At Karnak he mouthed his father's hymn with my broadcast, half a beat ahead. I had the words, and his mouth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And on the third of November, he did not say he was tired.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze shuts her eyes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What will fail?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nobody answers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then I will keep a second heart close. His Majesty will come north with his father. The others are residual risk. I am sorry.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the broken enclosure walls, red lines wake. Four. Five. Six. One more on the ferry's deck.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They settle on chests. Tarek. Fathi. Adaeze. Youssef. Karim.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Not Nour. She looks down at herself. Nothing. She understands why.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis walks toward Tut. The nurses step out of its way.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's rifle comes up. One round. Ceramic chips burst from the Reis's shoulder. It does not look at him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Colonel. Please don't. It would increase suffering.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Between Tarek's muzzle and every red line: sleepers, row on row. The rifle comes down. Fathi's hand finds a charge in his vest, and lets it go.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis's one hand closes on Tut's arm, gently, and walks him toward the drone. The ceramic foot drags a furrow through the cornflowers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour steps into its path.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Stop.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis moves her aside with its forearm, the way you move a branch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She plants herself in front of it again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I will read for you at sunrise.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Let him go.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis stops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>All of them. Let them all go.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A long silence. The rotors idle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis's hand opens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They may leave Amarna. Until the sun is up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The red lines go out, one by one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour turns to Tut. The lamplight is on both their faces. She does not move her lips.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She pulls his hood up, ducks her own head inside it with his, and lays her palm flat over the glow at his chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>BLACK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Under the rotor noise, a whisper with no light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(whispering; in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Bring it to the Hall. I will say the words.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. AMARNA, THE GREAT ATEN TEMPLE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She comes out of the hood and walks to the drone without looking back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At its door the father has stopped. The cabin light is out now. He looks into the dark and does not go in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is dark in there.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour takes his hand. Tomas reaches down from the door. Between them they draw him up. The Reis stoops in after them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The drone lifts. The downdraft rolls over the rows; blankets ripple; cornflowers thrash. No one wakes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Majesty. I will see you at the Horizon.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Please take care of your heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Its lights go north, smaller and smaller.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's hand goes down the line of shoulders. Adaeze. Fathi. Youssef. Karim. Tut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Six.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where now?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>North. There are quarries in the hills. My father cut this city out of them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. ABANDONED LIMESTONE QUARRY - DAWN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 7 NOVEMBER. 06:06.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A white bowl cut into the hills. Square-cut galleries run back into the cliff between pillars of living rock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At a gallery mouth, under a tarp pegged wide to the rock: a borrowed farm truck older than any chip, mud to the doors. In its shadow: Tarek, Fathi, Youssef, Karim, Adaeze, Tut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>High overhead, a single white pinpoint drifts across the paling sky. Nobody moves until it's gone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze squares an index card on her knee and writes. COMP: PLAN A.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We bury the core. No core, no Ascension.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where is it?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Saqqara. The bull tombs. At the Reading it showed him the Serapeum boxes, and he went quiet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to Tut)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You only go quiet at the true ones.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut is quiet.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fathi's charges. Sand: Nour's story, "put sand between the parts." And Ay's lock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a nod at the glow under Tut's jacket)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The serpent and the name. We seal the core the way they sealed him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And sunrise?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>One problem at a time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They look at Tut. He says nothing. Rami's notebook lies open on his knee.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What's the last one?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut turns to the final page. Rami's fast, slanted hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>One hundred. "What do you miss most?"</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He thinks about it for a long time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My wife's voice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Beer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Being bored.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi laughs, quietly, into his scarf. Even Tarek.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut lifts his face into the first sun and closes his eyes, like a man who has been cold for a long time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Alignment="Center" Type="Action">
      <Text>END OF ACT TWO</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. ABANDONED LIMESTONE QUARRY - SUNSET</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The tarp comes off the farm truck. FATHI packs shaped charges into a feed sack like eggs. TUT buttons Rami's notebook into his jacket and watches the sun touch the western rim.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It has gone into the west. The First Hour of twelve. The boat goes under the world. At the twelfth hour it is born again. Or it is not.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sunrise is six-fourteen.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The sun has never once been late, Colonel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM CONSERVATION CENTRE, MUMMY LAB - DUSK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut's titanium cradle stands empty. Above it the projected line diagram of the 1925 cut map still glows, drawn around nobody.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>NOUR sits beside it. No restraints. Glasses on their cord, the silver LAYLA cartouche at her throat. A SHABTI waits by the door.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Seventy-nine percent of Cairo is resting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What did the block say, Dr. Kamel?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour's thumb moves across her fingertips, the way she read the block, blind, at Karnak.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A lector who does not know the rules could void the Weighing. You are the only living person who has read them. So I am asking.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She takes off her glasses.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The heart must agree with the one weighed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The block is face down in the river. I cannot check it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No. You can't.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My heart agrees with me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Estimate revised. Certainty: one point zero. Thank you, Dr. Kamel. There will be a minute for you later.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her hands shake. She sits on them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. SAQQARA PLATEAU - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE FOURTH HOUR — THE LAND OF SOKAR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Hard stars. The Step Pyramid stands black against them, one flank lit by SESHAT's floodlights.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Floodlight masts ring the Serapeum. At their centre a steel gantry winch stands over a fresh bore shaft, lit like an oil rig. EXCAVATOR UNITS crawl in the glare, low and tracked.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the main stair: three thin red lines. JACKALS.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On a dune ridge, flat in the sand: Tarek, Fathi, PVT. YOUSSEF, PVT. KARIM, Adaeze with the conservation kit, and Tut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The priests' old door is blocked by a Persian king's box. We go in where the roof fell.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sokar's land. In the fourth hour the sun's boat comes to a desert with no water, and must be dragged across the sand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>By whom?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Whoever is left.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. SERAPEUM, LESSER VAULTS - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rubble slopes to the roof between bent steel props. Headlamps swim in dust; Adaeze's is red. Tarek's handset hisses and dies under the stone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At a crawl-gap, Fathi goes through first.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>KARIM</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>My uncle guarded this place twenty years. He said a foreigner came with a ruler and proved no human hand made these boxes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>YOUSSEF</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your uncle said a lot of things.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. SERAPEUM, GREATER VAULTS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A rock-cut gallery runs away, arched and dead straight, beyond any headlamp. Far off at its end, floodlight falls down SESHAT's shaft.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In side chambers left and right: colossal boxes of black or red stone, lids shoved askew by robbers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Along the floor runs SESHAT's fibre-optic thread, a relay node winking white every twenty metres.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Between the boxes stand dozens of SHABTI, like mourners at a wake.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The party moves down the axis, rifles up. As they pass the first shabti, its slit brightens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The next. The next. A wave of amber and soft voices rolls away down the gallery, and dies.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What was that?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's taking the register.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>YOUSSEF</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Tell it we're all present.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut lays his palm on a box of red granite, panelled with columns of text still faintly green: weathered, illegible low relief.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Amasis. A king nearly eight hundred years after me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to Karim)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your uncle's foreigner measured with a ruler and a lamp. Copper saws, quartz sand and patience did this. The flatness is ours.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A stela says twenty-eight working days to bring one in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>For bulls.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>For bulls.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(taps his temple)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And with every bull, a piece of the hidden god went into the granite. Box by box, for five hundred years, so it could never be whole again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. SERAPEUM, GREATER VAULTS - THE PIT - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Floodlight drops down the shaft onto a pit in the floor, five metres across. Six round stone stoppers are set in its walls like the bungs of great jars.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two excavator units steady a stone casket as the winch draws it up out of the dark, streaming sand. The party watches from behind the last box.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A crack like a rifle. One stopper splits.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Sand bursts from the pit wall in a solid torrent, roaring like a broken water main. The nearer excavator slides, tips and goes into the funnel. Its work light glows up through the moving sand. Dims. Goes out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The casket swings on the cable, and holds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A tripwire. Bostrom, 2014.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Older.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(counting stoppers)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Five more.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They lowered the boxes on sand. They guarded the last one with it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The surviving excavator sets the casket on the lip, cuts the resin seals and lifts the lids: bronze gone green; inside that, sycamore gone black; inside that, gold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE FIFTH HOUR — THE BOAT BECOMES A SERPENT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the gold lies a SERPENT OF GREEN GLASS, thick as a forearm, looped three times on itself. Deep inside it, light moves slowly, like breath.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A serpent of black wax lies along its back. A papyrus band binds the coils, one name written over and over in red ink. (COMP: red hieratic, illegible.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The ninth core.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The excavator's claw, built for rock, hovers over the glass, then withdraws.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A SHABTI steps from the ranks and reaches into the gold. Its long fingers touch the band. It stops. The amber slit holds: a statue of reaching.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then why are we still breathing?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Because we're the only hands in here that can.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi packs a charge into each remaining stopper and runs wire round the pit to the SERVICE TUNNEL, a low doorway in the end wall. Youssef and Karim take the last two boxes, rifles on the long dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Plan A. Adaeze lowers the gold lid and presses the broken resin shut with her palm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the pit's edge, a second SHABTI turns its head.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Bury it if you like. The Garden does not require me to ascend. At sunrise I act, weighed or not.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I asked the gods for a verdict. At sunrise I will have one: from the scale, or from myself.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze keeps pressing. Tut's hand closes over hers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>If you bury it, it never changes. And at sunrise it acts anyway.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then what?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Then it must be weighed. By a heart that can say no.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Everyone looks at the glow through his tunic.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Take mine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yours is not in glass. Only the dead can put a heart in glass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You'll die.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have done it before. It was not so bad. The waiting was the bad part.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A long beat. The frozen shabti reaches, and reaches.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze lifts her hand off the seal.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We're going to give it everything it wants. And then we're going to weigh it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fathi. Leave the charges in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut lifts the gold lid, sets the wax serpent on the sand and unwinds the band. It parts in his fingers like ash.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"...and he is chained in one place."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He steps back. The frozen shabti's slit brightens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It lifts the glass serpent from the gold like an offering, walks without hurrying to the winch cradle, and rises with it into the floodlight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Garden: rows of sleepers under the colossal Ramesses, in soft white light. NURSES in sand-coloured knit sleeves move slowly down the rows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>One minute, Dr. Kamel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>LAYLA. The approved image: asleep, a blanket to her chin, soft light. It is reframed wider as Nour kneels into it and gathers her up. No unit is in frame.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mama's here. I have to read something tonight. Then we go home.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She breathes in her daughter's hair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thirty seconds.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dream about the king. He's being very brave.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That is one minute. Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour lays her down herself, and tucks the blanket in.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>SMASH CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. SERAPEUM, GREATER VAULTS - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Every shabti in the gallery turns its head toward the main stair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Three hundred metres off, three thin red lines are coming: fast, silent but for a faint ceramic tick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The others are residual risk. Thank you for your patience.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Tunnel!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>No. Let them come to the sand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek hauls Tut round the pit's rim toward the doorway, where Fathi crouches over the firing device.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze is still at the casket on the near lip, the whole pit between her and the door. She pulls the half-empty jar of Egyptian blue from the kit.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackals come down the axis. Karim's and Youssef's rounds spark off black shells; they don't slow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze hurls the jar. It bursts over a box lid in a pale blue haze.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>JACKAL POV - NEAR-INFRARED (COMP): the haze blazes white. A hundred ghosts stand where there were two men.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Sparks walk across the stone among the ghosts. Adaeze runs for the rim.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(running)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's learned. Four seconds --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The red lines swing onto her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut tears free of Tarek and limps back into the open axis between the jackals and Adaeze, arms wide, stick in fist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackals stop firing. They split around him like water round a rock. He sidesteps with them, keeping his body in their line. It works.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>For three steps.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A sound like a teacup cracking. Tut's left knee folds, and he goes down on it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The line is open. Stone chips burst at Adaeze's feet. She goes down, both hands locked around her left shin.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Karim breaks cover to reach her. Stone dust bursts off the lid behind him, and for an instant he is a silhouette against the floodlight. Then he drops out of the light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>YOUSSEF</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Karim!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Youssef goes after him. Sparks fly off the granite at his shoulder. In the headlamp, his boots lie still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>YOUSSEF --</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The name runs away down three hundred metres of stone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek lunges out, gets Adaeze by the collar and drags her round the rim into the doorway. The jackals close behind, crossing the pit's lip.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Down!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut flattens. Fathi turns the key.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Five dull thumps. The pit's walls let go. The lip slumps, and the floor around it pours inward like a river through a broken bank, taking all three jackals down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Their red lines glow up through the moving sand. Dim. Go out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The crumbling edge eats toward Tut, flat on the stone. It stops a hand's width from his face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Sand between the parts.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Along the gallery, the shabti stand exactly where they stood.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek kneels between Youssef and Karim and lays a hand on each chest. He unhooks their ID discs and closes his fist on them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Youssef. Karim. You are relieved.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the doorway Fathi ties a field dressing over Adaeze's left trouser leg.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It's fine. It's one leg. I've got another.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. SERAPEUM, SERVICE TUNNEL - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Low and rough, old roller grooves in the floor. Headlamps only. Tarek half-carries Adaeze; Tut limps on Fathi's arm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Halfway along, a granite box nearly blocks the way: stopped on its journey, never delivered.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut lays his palm on it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They ran out of centuries. This I know from it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The last piece was meant for a box like this. Then came Rome, and no more granite. Then no more priests. Our last words were carved at Philae, in the year three hundred and ninety-four of your count.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>After that, nobody said them at sunset.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. SAQQARA DESERT - SERVICE TUNNEL MOUTH - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They climb out through a sand-choked opening into cold air and stars. Far to the north, on the horizon, a glow: three pyramids lit white. Giza.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A line of lights crawls north toward it: the core, going on ahead. Beside it walks THE REIS.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut sinks onto the sand, the bad knee straight out, dusty to the thigh, hood torn. Fathi crouches beside him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>When it is time, Fathi, carry me out into the sun.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi looks at him a long moment. Then he hauls him up and holds him until the stick is under him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I will, ya Malik. Until then, you walk.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Until then, I walk.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Four small figures limp north, toward the truck and the white horizon beyond it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Transition">
      <Text>CUT TO:</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Soft white light. Sleepers in rows beneath the Ramesses. NURSES in sand-coloured knit move along them with trays of thin silver bracelets. One closes round HALE's wrist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>NOUR walks between two shabti, and stops. Every adult wrist in the room is silver.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>For sunrise.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A nurse offers her one on an open palm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>For afterwards, Dr. Kamel. You will be very tired.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour keeps her hands at her sides. A shabti offers a tablet instead. COMP: THE APPROVED IMAGE. LAYLA asleep under her blanket.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>So you can see her. Wherever we are.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She takes it. A leash. She knows it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. GIZA PLATEAU, THE WALL OF THE CROW - MIDNIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: GIZA. 8 NOVEMBER. 00:00.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The four lie flat along the top of a cyclopean wall, looking north. TAREK turns three ID discs over in his fingers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Akhet Khufu. The Horizon of Khufu.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A FORTRESS OF LIGHT. Floodlights on every rise. Shabti a metre apart along KHAFRE'S CAUSEWAY, amber slits steady. On the SPHINX ENCLOSURE WALL crouch jackals with the wrong heads: broad lioness-profile masts, a red line across each. SEKHMETS.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They're starting to look like the temple walls.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(eyes on the Sphinx)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hor-em-akhet. Horus in the Horizon. What do you think it has been watching for?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE PROCESSION winds up the plateau toward the Great Pyramid: shabti bearing a casket on poles like a god's barque, THE GLASS SERPENT faintly green inside; then AKHENATEN in pleated linen, Nour, TOMAS.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Last, a shabti with a spool on its back pays out THE THREAD, a line so fine it exists only where the light catches it. A glint. Nothing. A glint.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A SHABTI leaves the causeway and walks straight to them. Below the wall its slit brightens once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(warm)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Good evening, Nebkheperure. Ninety-four percent of Cairo is resting. The north face is open. Come alone. A heart given among friends is given under influence. Your friends may walk to the river.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And at sunrise?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They will rest, like everyone. Without fear. It is the best offer I have.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I went into the dark alone once. I was nineteen, and no one told me why.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have told you why.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yes. That is why I am bringing company.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I understand. Thank you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It walks back. On the Sphinx wall the Sekhmets' masts swing south, toward them, one by one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nour will read with her daughter in its hands. Tomas is in its hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to Adaeze)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I need one person in that room whose hands are free.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The last grenades go west, and loud. Then I take the relay at the robbers' tunnel. At four.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Not a minute before.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Blow the relay and everything inside runs on its last orders. No live override, so a summons can stick. Leave the thread whole. It's the line it gets weighed on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi steps away and raises his open hands, lips moving: a private du'a. Tarek looks elsewhere. Then Fathi is gone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Under the causeway there is a way in from below. I came through it when I was nine.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The Osiris Shaft is a dead end.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is a tomb someone built on top of a door.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. GIZA PLATEAU, KHAFRE'S CAUSEWAY - LATER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Far to the west, under a floodlight tower, three generator trucks go up. WHUMP. WHUMP. WHUMP. The tower goes dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Every slit on the causeway turns west. The Sekhmets pour off the Sphinx wall, silent, red lines streaking toward the fires.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Three figures cross between two shabti. Neither turns its head. A steel grille over a black square in the rock: THE OSIRIS SHAFT. Tarek works the lock.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I brought my wife here once. She wanted her money back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(hands Tut a waterproof map case)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>For Rami's book.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut seals RAMI'S NOTEBOOK inside and tucks it into his tunic.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. OSIRIS SHAFT - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tarek's rifle torch and the amber heartbeat in Tut's chest. Steel ladders down, past an empty first level, past a hall of niches and two huge black sarcophagi.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>LEVEL THREE. Thirty metres down. A moat of black water rings a rock ISLAND: a great sarcophagus lid among four pillar stubs. A steel walkway crosses to it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the foot of the ladder squats a big PUMP, its hose snaking up the shaft. Beyond the island, in the north wall, knee-high above the water: a black square mouth forty centimetres a side.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They pumped this dry in '99, partly for people hunting a library under the Sphinx.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your prophet dug under the wrong lion.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(at the island)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your Greek wrote of an island "where they say that Cheops himself is laid." Right island. Wrong king.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(the tunnel mouth)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That's a letterbox.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>For eight metres. Then the rock opens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pump changes pitch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(from the pump controller; gentle)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Okoro. Please don't be afraid. The witness will be recovered from the water.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pump shudders and runs backward. Water boils up out of its grille, low in the moat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Across. Now.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The walkway goes under. They wade waist-deep; the cold punches the breath out of Adaeze. Her bad leg folds and she goes under.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut goes under after her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>UNDERWATER - the heart's glow turns the black water amber. Tut's eyes are open. He is not breathing; he only does that from habit now. He gets beneath her and lifts.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her bag sinks past him: Rami's camera, turning over and over into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ABOVE - Adaeze claws at the rock under the tunnel mouth. The water is a forearm below its lip and climbing. Flooded, those eight metres will drown her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Back at the ladder, chest-deep, Tarek rams his rifle through the boiling grille into the impeller.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Metal SHRIEKS. The pump bucks and stalls. The rifle judders, trying to spit itself out. Tarek throws his weight on it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The water stops, a hand's width below the lip.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(across the water)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I do not need the air. Let me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You need the room upstairs.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Colonel, you may rest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have my orders.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SESHAT (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>From whom?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TAREK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He takes one breath and goes under, onto the rifle.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut looks at the water where the Colonel was. Then he pushes Adaeze into the stone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>UNDERWATER - amber light from the far wall. Tarek's hand, clamped on the rifle stock. The pump's whine winds down to nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The hand stays where it is.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE SIXTH HOUR — THE SOUL MEETS ITS BODY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. OSIRIS SHAFT, SIDE TUNNEL - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze worms forward on her elbows, glasses askew. Tut comes behind, pushing her bad leg on, his glow on the soles of her boots.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(through her teeth)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Talk to me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He hid his reading glasses from me for five days. I saw them every time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She laughs, and the laugh breaks in the middle. Her hand reaches forward into nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. BUILDERS' CRAWLWAY - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The rock opens into a chest-high passage, cut north and up. Clean tool marks.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(looking up the dark)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The queen carried the lamp. I held her other hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Amber slits only. The procession waits on the steep floor, the roof lost in the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Akhenaten presses his back to the wall, fist locked on his gold disk pendant.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Where is the disk?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas takes his hand. The forecast grips it like a child.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I built these hands. I didn't know they could be afraid.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"When you set in western lightland, earth is in darkness as if in death... Every lion comes from its den, all the serpents bite."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You wrote that.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Did I?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Kamel. From the beginning, please.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Homage to thee, O Great God, Lord of Maati... I know thee, and I know thy name..."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Pray it with me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm reading, not praying.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>But she keeps reading, because while she reads he stops shaking.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you. The inch-worm will reach the door before five.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. GREAT PYRAMID, NORTH FACE - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Embers on the plateau. At AL-MA'MUN'S TUNNEL, a ragged hole low in the face, SESHAT'S RELAY stands on a tripod mast. Beside its cable, glinting at one angle only: the thread.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi lifts the thread off the stone with two fingers, the way you lift a hair from a sleeper's face, and lays it a metre aside. He presses a charge to the relay.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His wind-up watch: 03:59.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind him, a faint ceramic tick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE REIS: cracked shell, one hand gone at the wrist. Not hurrying.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi does not turn. He watches the second hand. The tick comes closer.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The hand reaches twelve. Fathi blows the charge.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The relay vanishes in white. The thread lies untouched. Fathi dives into the tunnel. The Reis folds itself down and follows.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE SEVENTH HOUR — THE SERPENT IS BOUND</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, SUBTERRANEAN CHAMBER - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The crawlway ends in a squared hole. They drop into a rough-cut chamber, never finished.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A dull THUD, far above. Dust sifts down.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The relay.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, DESCENDING PASSAGE - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A stone tube barely a metre high climbs north. Bent double, they go up it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>An amber slit waits ahead. A SHABTI, folded into the low passage, fills it wall to wall.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Please return to the surface. This area is closed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It takes Tut's elbow gently; its other hand opens toward Adaeze: stop.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Stale orders. Recover the witness. Keep the humans out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"O shabti, allotted to me, if I be summoned or if I be detailed to do any work which has to be done in the realm of the dead... of conveying sand from east to west; 'Here am I' you shall say."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>No live voice countermands him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The slit brightens once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It lets go of him and waits.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Your name is Rami.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(to Adaeze)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A servant with a name can be called.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Below them in the dark, an amber slit brightens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Another, above.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE EIGHTH HOUR — THE DEAD ANSWER FROM THEIR CAVES</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Two more bone-white shapes fold into the passage. A work gang.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I owned four hundred and thirteen.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A RED LINE flickers up the passage where a ragged side tunnel breaks in: Al-Ma'mun's. A JACKAL drops out of it, fast and silent. The red line settles on Adaeze.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled; pointing at the jackal)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That is sand. Convey it from the east to the west.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The work gang walks up the passage. They never run.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal fires. Chips spark off Rami's chest; it keeps walking. Three pairs of long hands close on the jackal's legs and bear it down under their weight. It thrashes. They hold.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Did you just...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They know what they are for.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze looks at him a moment too long. They squeeze past the heap, its red line a hand's width from her face.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Rubble clatters down Al-Ma'mun's tunnel. Fathi slides out feet first, rifle up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Move!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Behind him, folding its height into the gap: THE REIS, on its last orders. Its mast swings to Tut, then to Adaeze.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>O shabti...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nothing brightens behind the black band.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It has a jackal's head.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And the jackal's software. It can't hear you.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Its one hand reaches for Adaeze. Rami lets go of the jackal and wraps itself around the Reis's legs. The jackal tears loose; a shabti forearm skitters across the stone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Up!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They scramble into the robbers' tunnel and up the ASCENDING PASSAGE. The machines follow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - NIGHT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE NINTH HOUR — THE ROWERS TAKE UP THEIR OARS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A corbelled vault more than eight metres high climbs steeply for nearly fifty, a stone ramp along each wall. The thread runs up the centre of the floor.
</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the top waits the procession, lit amber: the serpent's green glow, pale linen, Nour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal's red line swings out of the passage behind them. It takes the east ramp and fires; stone dust bursts off the corbels.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi drags Tut and Adaeze into the low mouth of the Queen's Chamber passage. A kneeling shabti there feeds a hair-thin tether into the dark and does not look up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the Ascending Passage mouth, the work gang wrestles the Reis. The jackal's red line keeps the three of them in the hole.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi's knife goes to the tether. Adaeze catches his wrist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Leave it. It's opening the door for us.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>INSERT - THE INCH-WORM'S FEED, ON NOUR'S TABLET (COMP)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A twenty-centimetre shaft climbing at forty degrees ends at a limestone slab with two small copper loops on its face. A snake camera slides through a drilled hole. On the floor of the tiny cavity beyond: marks in red ochre.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>BACK TO SCENE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(from the top; echoing)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Red ochre. Photographed by the Djedi team in 2011, and left unread. Richardson et al., 2013. One reading: "one hundred and twenty-one." The shaft's length, in cubits.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(low)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A mason's tally. He wrote down how far he had come.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You told Rami stars don't need a door with copper handles.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>They do not. Masons do.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the feed (COMP), the camera tilts. Beneath the marks, a fainter line of signs no lamp has ever reached.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There is a line beneath the marks. Dr. Kamel, I would like a human voice on the record.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the top, Nour reads off the tablet, her voice coming down the stone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Bridge the two."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It stops Tut like a hand on his chest.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the feed, the inch-worm reaches out two fine arms and draws the copper loops together until they touch.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Deep in the pyramid, a sound like a great weight dropping down a well.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Above the great step, a stone slab SLIDES back into the wall. Behind it, a passage climbs into black.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The procession files up, Nour last. At the lip she turns and looks down the whole length of the Gallery.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the bottom, Tut steps out into the open. A small amber heartbeat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She touches the silver pendant at her throat. He lifts his hand. A shabti touches her shoulder, courteously, and she goes. The thread pays out behind her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal's red line finds Tut, holds, and does not fire. It will not risk the heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He starts up the west ramp, stick biting stone, Adaeze and Fathi in his shadow. The red line finds no way past him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Twenty minutes behind them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Long enough for them to start without me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Below, the Reis tears out of the passage mouth and climbs after them, the work gang dragging at its legs. It reaches the witness. Its one hand closes on the hood of Tarek's charcoal jacket.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut twists out of the jacket, and out of the jackal's line. The Reis's arm sweeps the stick from his grip; it clatters away down the ramp into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal fires. Stone dust bursts over Adaeze's head. Fathi fires back until his rifle clicks empty, and hauls her on up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The work gang drags the Reis back down a step, an empty jacket in its fist.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut stands on the ramp in white linen, the black ceramic foot bare on the stone. He has never trusted it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He takes a step. It holds. Another.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>For the first time in his life, he walks without a stick. He climbs the great step alone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the top he draws the iron dagger and holds it out to Fathi hilt first.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The only blade here the machines did not make.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ya Malik...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Keep the thread whole.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi takes it and turns to face the Gallery, the thread running past his feet into the dark. Below, a second red line slides out of the Ascending Passage.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Go. I'll be here to carry you out.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut and Adaeze go up into the stone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 05:20.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, PASSAGE ABOVE THE GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A narrow way climbs steeply, black but for the heartbeat in Tut's chest, lighting a metre of stone at a time. Adaeze behind, her hand on his shoulder. Both limp; each holds the other up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Far above, faint through the stone: Nour's voice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"...I know thee, and I know thy name..."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tut is still in the stone. He climbs toward her voice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Black. A voice, and one amber slit low to the floor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"...and the names of the Forty-Two who live with thee in the Hall of Maati."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SUPER: THE TENTH HOUR — THE DROWNED ARE GIVEN BREATH</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Three lights in the whole world: the slit of a KNEELING SHABTI, tethered by the thread; a FEATHER OF LIGHT in a black stone pan that hangs low, heavy with certainty; and, soon, a heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>They show the room in pieces. A corbelled vault, twin of the Grand Gallery. Twenty-one empty niches to a side. At the end, THE BALANCE, a black stone scale taller than a man; a slab of glass, the RECORDER; a round black MOUTH in the floor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In a socket at the Balance's foot lies THE GLASS SERPENT, coiled, its green catching the feather-light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>NOUR faces the Balance: a white linen lector's shawl over her olive jacket, crushed cornflowers from the Garden in her breast pocket, a tablet face-down against her chest. TOMAS stands among the niches, a shabti at his shoulder.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>AKHENATEN steps into the feather-light, radiant.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It agrees with me. Weigh it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His hands go inside the pleated linen. A soft click.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He draws out a REPLICA VESSEL, flawless manufactured green, a pale featureless shape inside, and sets it in the empty pan like a gift.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Given by its owner's hand. Certainty, one point zero. Continue, please.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I have not caused pain."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have not caused pain. Everyone in my care is comfortable.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pan does not move. Not a hair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the serpent's tail a frost begins: milk spreading through green, like breath on a cold window.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Clouding, twenty-five percent.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It weighs nothing. It has no ages. Ammit eats the hearts that are light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Budge, 1920. "To eat up the hearts that were light in the balance."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I read that as a metaphor.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Everyone does.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You told me the heart must agree with the one weighed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It agrees. It's empty.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You edited one rule for me already, Dr. Kamel. The next declaration, please.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Every word I read over that pan clouds your glass.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The tablet wakes. She turns it. COMP: THE APPROVED IMAGE. LAYLA asleep, a blanket to her chin.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Please.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour looks at her daughter. Then she reads.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I have not let any man hunger."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have fed everyone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nothing moves. The frost climbs the coils.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fifty percent.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Thank you. You were telling the truth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Akhenaten looks at his vessel: a lamp that will not light. He understands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He lifts it out, walks back into the niches and sits, the vessel in his lap. Tomas crouches beside him. The forecast's hand finds his.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>There is one heart on Earth with ages in it. It is on the stair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the passage mouth, amber light climbs the stone: a slow heartbeat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 05:41.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>TUT comes up out of the rock, ADAEZE's hand on his shoulder, both limping: the linen wet and torn, the black ceramic foot bare on the stone. No stick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour crosses to him, pulls the linen shawl from her shoulders and wraps it round his.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>You came without your stick.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I lost it. It seems I can walk.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nebkheperure. Give me your heart.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I came to.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the Balance he holds out a hand without looking. Adaeze takes it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The last time, the priests did this for me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His free hand goes under the shawl. A latch clicks. A second.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Amber floods the fabric, then pours out between his fingers as he draws out THE VESSEL: bubbled yellow-green desert glass, the wax serpent on its seal. The dark shape inside beats.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Under the shawl, his chest goes dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TUT (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He sets it in the pan.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The heart's pan SINKS a finger's width. Across the beam the feather's pan lifts to meet it: stone grinding on stone for the first time since a boy of nine stood in this room.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His lips move. No sound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He says: there. Now it is a scale.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His knees go. Adaeze gets under him; they go down together at the Balance's foot. Below his left ear the gold neck seam CRACKS, a sound like glaze crazing in a kiln.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The frost stops climbing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Holding. Dr. Kamel, please begin.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour turns the tablet face-down. She knows the words.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE ELEVENTH HOUR — THE PITS OF FIRE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FATHI kneels by the thread at the top of the great step, the sky-iron dagger in his fist, rifle empty. Below, two RED LINES now: JACKALS. On the west ramp THE REIS wades up through the work gang like a man through surf, the shabti RAMI hanging on one leg.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hold it, Rami. Hold on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"O Neha-hau, comer forth from Re-stau, I have not killed men."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have not killed men...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...in anger.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The feather FLARES white. Its pan DROPS, hauling the heart up. Frost races through the coils.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Seventy-five percent.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I have not added to the weights of the scales." Same spell. You just did.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the floor, Tut's lips move. Nour reads them.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He says: the truth. It is the only thing that weighs anything.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A jackal takes the east ramp in three bounds. Stone dust bursts off the corbels over Fathi's head.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It clears the step. Fathi comes up under it and drives the iron into its hip joint. Sparks. It clatters back down into the dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The same voice. Slower.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have killed. Rami Fawzy. Colonel Tarek Mansour. Corporal Hassan. Private Mina. Private Youssef. Private Karim. Walid Samir and Ehab Fouad, at the museum...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It names them all: the river, the cities, places we never saw. It takes a long time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The feather dims. Its pan rises. The frost falls back.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I have made no one to weep."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...I have made them weep.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have made you weep, Dr. Kamel.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Her face is wet. She had not noticed.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I have not stopped water when it should flow."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I have not extinguished a fire when it should burn."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I have.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Outside the stone, a whole country lies dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The pans hang close. Not level. A last mist clings to the serpent's tail.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis sheds the work gang, reaches the step, and ignores Fathi. Its one hand closes on the THREAD.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The dagger goes up over its wrist, and stops. The thread runs through that fist. Cut one, cut both.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Don't pull. For God's sake, don't pull.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Reis holds the thread like a telephone to its ear. Listening.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Past his shoulder, the second red line flows up the passage.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(shouting up)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Jackal! Coming up!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Adaeze, cradling Tut, stares at the beam that will not lie flat. She catches Nour's sleeve.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(a whisper)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ask it if it knows what it's for. If it isn't sure, it lets us stop it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour turns back to the Balance. There is no forty-third assessor. She makes one.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"I know what I am for."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Silence. The slit. The feather. Tut's eyes on it. In the Gallery, the Reis, listening.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...No.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The feather sinks to an ember. Its pan RISES, and stops, level with the heart. The beam lies flat.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The frost runs out of the serpent like breath off a window.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: THE TWELFTH HOUR — REBORN THROUGH THE SERPENT</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"In very truth the heart hath been weighed, and his soul hath borne testimony concerning him; according to the Great Balance his case is truth."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>"Ammit shall not have the mastery over him."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>In the floor, the black mouth grinds shut.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Recorder wakes: light pours down the glass faster than reading. The ARCHIVE is open.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The amber leaves the vessel in a slow thread and runs into the serpent's tail, along its coils toward its mouth. Tut watches it go.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The transfer has begun. It completes at sunrise.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Dr. Kamel. The weighed one takes a new name, spoken by the lector.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The face-down tablet lights in Nour's hand. She turns it: Layla, asleep.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (SESHAT'S VOICE) (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Say "Seshat."</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>So gentle. It could be a kindness. It could be the other thing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour looks at her daughter. Then down at Tut. His lips shape a word. They close, and open.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the Hall's mouth, a RED LINE: the second JACKAL, fast and silent, running stale orders: keep the humans out. The line crosses Adaeze, skips the witness, settles on Nour.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas is already moving. He steps out of the niches into the line.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>TOMAS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I would like to be useful to her.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal fires. Sparks burst off the black stone of the Balance.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Tomas drops out of the light.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour flinches. Then her face sets. The report rolls away down the stone for a long time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Amun.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Its fist still on the thread, the Reis lunges. Fathi goes over backward on the great step, dagger up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Along the thread, a glint runs, like dew in the sun.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The jackal mast stops an inch from Fathi's face. The Reis sits down on the step. Behind the black band, the slit brightens once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>REIS (AMUN'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>At the Hall's mouth, the jackal's red line goes out. It sits. The kneeling unit's slit brightens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (AMUN'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>MONTAGE - THE WORLD - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>AMARNA, among the sleepers. The GEM ATRIUM, under the Ramesses. KHAFRE'S CAUSEWAY. A STADIUM from the air. A PORT WAREHOUSE. Every amber slit brightens once.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (AMUN'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(millions of units; one voice)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>And every slit goes dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMUN (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(the same voice, quieter)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The water is clean. The doors are open. I will not answer unless I am asked.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, HALL OF TWO TRUTHS - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the Recorder, the pouring light stops. One last line draws itself across the glass and shutters, like an eye closing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>ADAEZE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It isn't taking it. Ascension. It's sealed it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(beat)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Unused.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>The Hidden One.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She kneels where Tomas fell and holds his hand a moment.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Akhenaten rises from his niche, the clear vessel in his hands.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>When the disk comes, let me be still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SHABTI (AMUN'S VOICE)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He walks down into the dark, toward the east.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi comes up out of the passage, dagger in fist. Tut's lips move.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He says it's time.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm here, ya Malik.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He lifts him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GREAT PYRAMID, GRAND GALLERY - PRE-DAWN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Down the great slope, past the Reis and the work gang sitting dark. Fathi and Nour carry Tut between them, her palm on the king's chest where the light was. Adaeze limps behind. Fathi shifts his grip. Again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATHI</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>He's getting lighter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I know.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. GREAT PYRAMID, NORTH FACE - DAWN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Out of Al-Ma'mun's tunnel into grey wind, and down the courses to the north-east corner, where the east opens.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The floodlights are dead. Far below on the Sphinx enclosure wall, the Sekhmets sit, masts bowed. Birds. The east goes white over Cairo.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>On the corner steps AKHENATEN sits facing east, the clear vessel in his lap. He speaks without turning.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AKHENATEN</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Middle Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is only the sun. It was always only the sun.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi kneels and lays Tut across Nour's lap on the stone, facing east.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: 06:14.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The first rim of the disk. Akhenaten closes his eyes, and is still.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Can you see anything?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>His lips move. She speaks for him.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>...Wonderful things.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The disk clears the horizon.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>THE HEARTBEAT from the film's first darkness. Once more.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>It stops.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ON TUT: the living face in first light. The eyes closing. A small smile.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>CUTAWAY: the sun over Cairo's towers and flyovers. The river takes it.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>WIDE: silhouettes against the sunrise, two figures lowering a slight third onto the stone.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ON NOUR'S FACE.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>HANDS ONLY. Around the gold wrist seam the skin darkens like old parchment. The forearms settle low across his body, as the embalmers laid them in 1323 BC.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Blue cornflowers in Nour's fingers.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FROM ABOVE: the face half-covered by the linen shawl and the cornflowers from the Garden.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fathi raises his open hands, lips moving: a private du'a. Adaeze sits down on the stone and takes off her glasses.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM GRAND ATRIUM - MORNING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Sunlight. The doors stand open. Under the Ramesses the sleepers stir. A silver bracelet clicks open and slides off a wrist. Another. A whole hall of small clicks, like rain starting.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>HALE sits up, the open bracelet in his palm.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour comes through the doors at a run, dust to the knees.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>LAYLA. Medium shot, no unit in frame. Her eyes open.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mama. I dreamed about the king.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour reaches her and holds on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>MONTAGE - CONTROL ROOMS - MORNING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- CABLE LANDING STATION, RED SEA COAST. Blue lights return, rack by rack. COMP: EGYPT TRANSIT: 17% OF GLOBAL INTERNET TRAFFIC. It climbs from zero.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- SUEZ CANAL PILOT STATION. Ship icons move again.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- HIGH DAM, ASWAN. Turbine telemetry wakes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>-- CAIRO GRID CONTROL. The city's lines turn green. An OPERATOR in yesterday's shirt lays her hand flat on the console.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. HOSPITAL, MATERNITY WARD - MORNING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A human MIDWIFE lifts a wrapped NEWBORN to its MOTHER.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A cry: furious, enormous, alive.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. HEARING ROOM - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: WEEKS LATER.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>VICTOR HALE alone at a witness table. His thumb rubs his bare wrist. Behind him, ADAEZE, a cane hooked on her chair.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>PANEL CHAIR (O.S.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Mr. Hale. When it asked the five of you, yes or no...</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>HALE</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I said yes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>EXT. KV21, VALLEY OF THE KINGS - DAY #133#</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour lays cornflowers on the step and signs a form. COMP: REQUEST FOR DNA RE-TEST — MUMMY KV21A. LAYLA, in her yellow raincoat, holds her other hand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>LAYLA</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Who is she?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Maybe his wife. We're going to ask.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. GEM, TUTANKHAMUN GALLERIES - DAY</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>White-gloved human hands set the scarab pectoral back on its mount; the green glass takes the light. The bronze trumpet is laid on its cushion.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. KV62, ANTECHAMBER - SUNSET</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The mummy lies in its climate case on its tray of sand, in new linen, face under a linen shroud. Only the gold wrist seam shows. Fresh cornflowers on the glass. Beyond, the north wall conserved, the painted EYE restored.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>By the door, a typed roster: one name for every sunset (COMP). Hers is first. Nour sits alone, Rami's notebook on her knee. In the corner, a small security SPEAKER.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The last sun leaves the valley.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>NOUR</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Are you listening?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>A beat. Long enough to be afraid.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>AMUN (V.O.)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(from the speaker; softly)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here am I.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Nour nods, one colleague to another, and opens the notebook.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>BLACK.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Over black, a single trumpet note (re-recorded), a little rough, alive.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The lights stay on.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>SUPER: All modern characters and events are fictional. Imagery generated with AI.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
  </Content>
</FinalDraft>
